WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.970
&gt;&gt; Welcome everyone to

00:00:05.971 --> 00:00:07.770
this session on the
good bad and the ugly

00:00:07.771 --> 00:00:10.530
of ML in network systems.

00:00:10.531 --> 00:00:12.750
Applying ML to a problem needs

00:00:12.751 --> 00:00:15.310
no real motivation
or push in 2018.

00:00:15.311 --> 00:00:17.775
We're seeing a huge surge
in developing

00:00:17.776 --> 00:00:20.880
ML techniques for a lot of
large-scale network systems.

00:00:20.881 --> 00:00:22.560
With the huge amount

00:00:22.561 --> 00:00:24.240
of data and computing
power available,

00:00:24.241 --> 00:00:26.460
this actually makes
it quite feasible.

00:00:26.461 --> 00:00:28.650
Of course, Microsoft has been in

00:00:28.651 --> 00:00:30.705
the forefront of applying

00:00:30.706 --> 00:00:35.080
data at huge scales to
large-scale systems issues.

00:00:35.081 --> 00:00:38.390
However, there is
a growing voice,

00:00:38.391 --> 00:00:40.100
maybe even the silent majority of

00:00:40.101 --> 00:00:42.945
voices in the systems and
networking community,

00:00:42.946 --> 00:00:46.110
that is clamoring for
more principal solutions.

00:00:46.111 --> 00:00:48.410
They're concerned
that they're building

00:00:48.411 --> 00:00:50.180
solutions without
quite understanding

00:00:50.181 --> 00:00:51.890
what they actually do.

00:00:51.891 --> 00:00:56.510
This session intends to
write into this to stop.

00:00:56.511 --> 00:01:01.335
What are the network systems
for which ML is appropriate?

00:01:01.336 --> 00:01:03.170
The fact that we have built

00:01:03.171 --> 00:01:05.385
systems are built
with abstractions,

00:01:05.386 --> 00:01:07.460
does it make it more amenable to

00:01:07.461 --> 00:01:10.240
using black-box
ML-based techniques?

00:01:10.241 --> 00:01:13.610
Finally, do we even need
full understanding of

00:01:13.611 --> 00:01:17.505
the solutions as the so-called
traditionalists insist?

00:01:17.506 --> 00:01:20.235
To discuss all these
questions and more,

00:01:20.236 --> 00:01:26.180
we have a panel of
researchers in this space.

00:01:26.181 --> 00:01:30.520
We'll start with Balaji
Prabhakar from Stanford.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.850
Balaji is a professor
at Stanford.

00:01:33.851 --> 00:01:35.930
His research is largely in

00:01:35.931 --> 00:01:39.430
the space of
self-programming networks,

00:01:39.431 --> 00:01:41.420
particularly in the context of

00:01:41.421 --> 00:01:45.080
cloud computing platforms
and data centers.

00:01:45.081 --> 00:01:47.940
Balaji has a long list
of awards but I won't

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:50.860
list all of them but I would
just note that is one,

00:01:50.861 --> 00:01:53.595
the Erlang prize from
the Applied Probability Society,

00:01:53.596 --> 00:01:55.395
and the Rollo Davidson Prize

00:01:55.396 --> 00:01:57.250
from the University of Cambridge.

00:01:57.251 --> 00:01:58.890
He's a Sloan fellow,

00:01:58.891 --> 00:02:02.160
Itripoli and ACM fellow as well.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:07.550
&gt;&gt; Thank you. Hi, good
afternoon everybody.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:13.520
I'm going to take a position
and my main thesis is that,

00:02:13.521 --> 00:02:17.185
on the good bad ugly side,
I'm going to say it's good.

00:02:17.186 --> 00:02:20.985
What's ugly and sometimes
can be bad is the hype,

00:02:20.986 --> 00:02:22.445
but in of themselves,

00:02:22.446 --> 00:02:25.025
there's nothing wrong with ideas,

00:02:25.026 --> 00:02:28.220
is how we end up
using them or worse,

00:02:28.221 --> 00:02:32.720
how we end up making
a bigger dealer of them.

00:02:32.721 --> 00:02:36.270
So, some of my background
is theoretical,

00:02:36.271 --> 00:02:38.464
and so the scientific
modelling process

00:02:38.465 --> 00:02:40.395
always goes on a knife edge.

00:02:40.396 --> 00:02:43.355
There are few theorems
that give universality,

00:02:43.356 --> 00:02:45.065
but other than that,

00:02:45.066 --> 00:02:48.275
things tend to be
for tractability.

00:02:48.276 --> 00:02:53.480
We make some models and
they work by and large.

00:02:53.481 --> 00:02:55.580
There's some well-known
combinations,

00:02:55.581 --> 00:02:59.270
like linear systems go with
Gaussian noise really well,

00:02:59.271 --> 00:03:01.745
and that goes with quadratic
cost functions really well.

00:03:01.746 --> 00:03:05.270
That's a fairly large amount
of optimal control theory,

00:03:05.271 --> 00:03:08.305
stochastic control, runs on
those three assumptions.

00:03:08.306 --> 00:03:11.415
People have tried relaxing

00:03:11.416 --> 00:03:13.855
any one of these and
it's not been easy.

00:03:13.856 --> 00:03:16.370
Nobody's heard from those people

00:03:16.371 --> 00:03:18.575
who've tried to relax
these assumptions.

00:03:18.576 --> 00:03:21.345
They've gone off into
the woods and never come back.

00:03:21.346 --> 00:03:24.395
Similarly, networking
we know there is again

00:03:24.396 --> 00:03:26.780
Markovian assumption
whether it's to poisson

00:03:26.781 --> 00:03:29.550
arrivals and exponential
services small variations but,

00:03:29.551 --> 00:03:31.100
otherwise, by and large,

00:03:31.101 --> 00:03:33.885
that's a successful
modeling class.

00:03:33.886 --> 00:03:37.130
What happens when we
leave this world of

00:03:37.131 --> 00:03:40.160
modeling assumptions
don't quite work

00:03:40.161 --> 00:03:43.900
or not want to capture
something in more exact detail?

00:03:43.901 --> 00:03:49.070
That is where lots of
data in these days,

00:03:49.071 --> 00:03:51.355
and when combined with

00:03:51.356 --> 00:03:55.515
neural networks and
ML, can actually help.

00:03:55.516 --> 00:03:59.475
So, it helps us to capture
something that is actual,

00:03:59.476 --> 00:04:01.610
an actual system where

00:04:01.611 --> 00:04:04.780
the modeling assumptions
have gone some distance,

00:04:04.781 --> 00:04:08.420
but now, we want to be
more exact than that.

00:04:08.421 --> 00:04:11.060
What that does is it
does certainly leave

00:04:11.061 --> 00:04:13.565
the burden of
understanding upon us,

00:04:13.566 --> 00:04:16.160
and I'm okay if

00:04:16.161 --> 00:04:18.330
first we get success
in that understanding,

00:04:18.331 --> 00:04:19.960
usually prefers
understanding first,

00:04:19.961 --> 00:04:24.110
and then success, then we can
declare success correctly.

00:04:24.111 --> 00:04:26.360
But people have had

00:04:26.361 --> 00:04:27.460
observations that for which

00:04:27.461 --> 00:04:29.675
theories will come
later, that's fine.

00:04:29.676 --> 00:04:31.670
It's what just saying sharing

00:04:31.671 --> 00:04:33.570
an anecdote with Niels Bohr,

00:04:33.571 --> 00:04:36.435
about Niels Bohr
or involving him.

00:04:36.436 --> 00:04:40.340
He had a scientific
friend visit with

00:04:40.341 --> 00:04:42.500
him at some summer home

00:04:42.501 --> 00:04:44.880
that he had in
the mountains somewhere,

00:04:44.881 --> 00:04:47.360
and banged on the door
frame of his house.

00:04:47.361 --> 00:04:50.060
There was horseshoe,

00:04:50.061 --> 00:04:52.910
which is well-known
as a good luck charm.

00:04:52.911 --> 00:04:55.095
So, scientific friend
said, "Surely,

00:04:55.096 --> 00:04:57.880
Professor Bohr, you don't
believe in this superstition."

00:04:57.881 --> 00:04:59.685
He said, "Well, I
don't believe in it,

00:04:59.686 --> 00:05:01.090
but they say it works,

00:05:01.091 --> 00:05:03.120
whether you believe
in it or not."

00:05:03.121 --> 00:05:05.405
It's not a bad thing
to keep in mind

00:05:05.406 --> 00:05:08.240
for the topic of this panel.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:13.560
I want to take two specific uses
for neural networks.

00:05:13.561 --> 00:05:16.325
First this so-called
function approximation.

00:05:16.326 --> 00:05:18.080
The well known to
have the property

00:05:18.081 --> 00:05:19.710
of approximating functions.

00:05:19.711 --> 00:05:21.620
A two layer neural
networks are universal

00:05:21.621 --> 00:05:24.690
as long ago theorem
of Andrew Barons.

00:05:24.691 --> 00:05:29.880
Second is that, when combined
with hardware of the GPU,

00:05:29.881 --> 00:05:31.880
TPU type, this ability

00:05:31.881 --> 00:05:34.430
to approximate a function
can vastly speed up,

00:05:34.431 --> 00:05:36.185
and was very useful
for us in networking.

00:05:36.186 --> 00:05:38.005
Speed up computations,

00:05:38.006 --> 00:05:39.490
I'm going to give
you some examples.

00:05:39.491 --> 00:05:41.930
One example supporting both

00:05:41.931 --> 00:05:43.400
is from self-programming
networks.

00:05:43.401 --> 00:05:44.900
So, I'll tell you what that is,

00:05:44.901 --> 00:05:46.130
and briefly also tell

00:05:46.131 --> 00:05:47.540
you some work I've

00:05:47.541 --> 00:05:49.190
been doing with the
students Sachin Katti,

00:05:49.191 --> 00:05:52.760
Mani Kotaru, and also
Gordon Wetzstein

00:05:52.761 --> 00:05:57.190
who's a colleague who specialize
in VR computer vision.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:05:59.410
Finally, I was at

00:05:59.411 --> 00:06:03.325
the Simons Institute Program
in Berkeley last season,

00:06:03.326 --> 00:06:06.325
last spring in the one
that is run by,

00:06:06.326 --> 00:06:09.580
and we had a physicist from

00:06:09.581 --> 00:06:12.820
Caltech who's involved in
elementary particle physics,

00:06:12.821 --> 00:06:14.455
this large hadron collider,

00:06:14.456 --> 00:06:16.220
and they also use neural networks

00:06:16.221 --> 00:06:17.810
and pretty much the same way
that I'm going

00:06:17.811 --> 00:06:21.325
to describe it for
the same reasons also.

00:06:21.326 --> 00:06:22.710
So, self-programming networks as

00:06:22.711 --> 00:06:25.165
a research program on Stanford,

00:06:25.166 --> 00:06:27.815
Mendel Rosenblum and I are

00:06:27.816 --> 00:06:30.120
working on it together
with a bunch of students,

00:06:30.121 --> 00:06:33.050
and the infrared learners are

00:06:33.051 --> 00:06:34.475
blue because that's where

00:06:34.476 --> 00:06:36.985
we've used some neural networks.

00:06:36.986 --> 00:06:40.470
So, again, what is self-driving,

00:06:40.471 --> 00:06:42.780
self-programming networks.

00:06:42.781 --> 00:06:45.080
So, think of a self-driving car,

00:06:45.081 --> 00:06:46.560
you take a plain old car.

00:06:46.561 --> 00:06:48.755
If you add sense
and control to it,

00:06:48.756 --> 00:06:50.970
then it becomes Stanley
the self-driving car,

00:06:50.971 --> 00:06:56.020
this is Sebastian thrown the
on the trunk under the hood.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:59.560
Similarly, we would take
a plain old data center,

00:06:59.561 --> 00:07:01.570
and our focus is on data centers.

00:07:01.571 --> 00:07:03.590
So, in other words,
I'm not advocating

00:07:03.591 --> 00:07:04.790
what we're doing for

00:07:04.791 --> 00:07:06.620
wider Internet yet because

00:07:06.621 --> 00:07:08.830
there's some particular
assumptions we made.

00:07:08.831 --> 00:07:11.110
You have sense and control to it,

00:07:11.111 --> 00:07:12.880
and we want to use that

00:07:12.881 --> 00:07:15.580
to form a self-programming
networks.

00:07:15.581 --> 00:07:17.030
I don't have time
to tell you more

00:07:17.031 --> 00:07:18.300
about it, where we sense,

00:07:18.301 --> 00:07:23.230
how we control, its mostly
is NIC-based approach.

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:25.910
What that allows us to do is

00:07:25.911 --> 00:07:27.350
to run some new applications that

00:07:27.351 --> 00:07:30.410
can run on the old
plain old data centers,

00:07:30.411 --> 00:07:31.775
that allows us to run this

00:07:31.776 --> 00:07:33.200
because it has
additional functionality,

00:07:33.201 --> 00:07:36.020
like very accurate timestamping
service and so on.

00:07:36.021 --> 00:07:37.735
So, in this wall,

00:07:37.736 --> 00:07:39.980
neural networks, I
want to get to it.

00:07:39.981 --> 00:07:42.950
Comes in the following
way: We've taken

00:07:42.951 --> 00:07:46.380
a thermography type approach
to measurement.

00:07:46.381 --> 00:07:50.590
I look at ingress and egress
times of packets at NICs.

00:07:50.591 --> 00:07:52.850
When you have this for
a whole bunch of packets,

00:07:52.851 --> 00:07:53.870
you're in the world of solving

00:07:53.871 --> 00:07:55.760
simultaneous linear equations to

00:07:55.761 --> 00:07:57.620
determine from the total time

00:07:57.621 --> 00:08:00.270
how much time was
spent in each box.

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:07.770
The algorithm for this is
LASSO, the one we use.

00:08:07.771 --> 00:08:09.420
So, you're looking
at the total delay,

00:08:09.421 --> 00:08:11.730
D. A is the adjacency matrix

00:08:11.731 --> 00:08:14.105
that decides what path
the packet took,

00:08:14.106 --> 00:08:16.120
Q is the queuing delays
of the switches its

00:08:16.121 --> 00:08:18.700
visiting, N is the noise.

00:08:18.701 --> 00:08:21.640
Noise is variable wireless,

00:08:21.641 --> 00:08:24.350
NIC timestamping inaccuracy,
various things like this.

00:08:24.351 --> 00:08:27.520
We worked on this and tested
it in Google's 40G-test bed,

00:08:27.521 --> 00:08:29.800
the internal production network
and various other places,

00:08:29.801 --> 00:08:31.610
our Internet data
center at Stanford,

00:08:31.611 --> 00:08:32.980
but this is the algorithm.

00:08:32.981 --> 00:08:35.380
So, given the end-to-end delay,

00:08:35.381 --> 00:08:40.355
estimate individual queuing
delays or a certain interval.

00:08:40.356 --> 00:08:43.580
This works, but with

00:08:43.581 --> 00:08:45.630
slow LASSO algorithm scales

00:08:45.631 --> 00:08:48.225
poorly with the size
of the network.

00:08:48.226 --> 00:08:52.880
There are faster implementations
of LASSO but even so,

00:08:52.881 --> 00:08:54.740
we want to see if we can just

00:08:54.741 --> 00:08:56.485
teach a neural
network to do this.

00:08:56.486 --> 00:09:00.120
What that means is, take
an algorithm like LASSO,

00:09:00.121 --> 00:09:03.030
it has it's input
output map that defines

00:09:03.031 --> 00:09:07.335
a function and take
the input-output pairs,

00:09:07.336 --> 00:09:09.010
lots and lots and lots of them,

00:09:09.011 --> 00:09:11.055
and train a neural
network with it.

00:09:11.056 --> 00:09:12.920
You have to remember after all,

00:09:12.921 --> 00:09:15.175
we're just inverting noisy
in linear equations.

00:09:15.176 --> 00:09:17.690
If there's no noise in
that equation well-determined,

00:09:17.691 --> 00:09:20.150
it was just another
linear system.

00:09:20.151 --> 00:09:22.390
So, you expect a

00:09:22.391 --> 00:09:24.665
priori the Neural Networks
should have a good chance.

00:09:24.666 --> 00:09:26.540
So, it turns out just a two layer

00:09:26.541 --> 00:09:28.835
is completely as
shallow as you can get,

00:09:28.836 --> 00:09:33.910
a Neural Network of the basic
ReLU type is good enough.

00:09:33.911 --> 00:09:37.040
So, what's the result look like?

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:40.700
On the top is
Reconstruction Accuracy.

00:09:40.701 --> 00:09:42.315
This first chart.

00:09:42.316 --> 00:09:45.745
You can have different
size networks.

00:09:45.746 --> 00:09:48.695
LASSO has a root-mean-squared
error which is,

00:09:48.696 --> 00:09:49.750
you can think of it as how

00:09:49.751 --> 00:09:51.145
many packets like roughly three

00:09:51.146 --> 00:09:53.100
full-sized for
general byte packets

00:09:53.101 --> 00:09:55.005
roughly, that's the error.

00:09:55.006 --> 00:09:56.970
The second column is
Neural Networks trained

00:09:56.971 --> 00:09:59.295
with ground truth when
you have it available.

00:09:59.296 --> 00:10:00.950
Third is to
Neural Network trained

00:10:00.951 --> 00:10:03.180
with the output of LASSO itself.

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:05.590
So, as you can see,

00:10:05.591 --> 00:10:09.270
it's pretty accurate but it
is not much loss of accuracy.

00:10:09.271 --> 00:10:11.640
But the killer is
the second thing.

00:10:11.641 --> 00:10:14.020
LASSO scales poorly
as I said before,

00:10:14.021 --> 00:10:15.460
30 milliseconds worth of

00:10:15.461 --> 00:10:17.150
data takes you more
than a millisecond,

00:10:17.151 --> 00:10:18.190
depending on size
of the network, you

00:10:18.191 --> 00:10:19.705
can get as bad as a second.

00:10:19.706 --> 00:10:22.330
But if we're trying
Neural Network,

00:10:22.331 --> 00:10:26.020
we can still be tracking time.

00:10:26.021 --> 00:10:27.510
So, that's an example

00:10:27.511 --> 00:10:29.870
of function
approximation capability

00:10:29.871 --> 00:10:32.500
as well as hardware acceleration.

00:10:32.501 --> 00:10:34.525
So, that's my first example.

00:10:34.526 --> 00:10:37.480
Second one is again,

00:10:37.481 --> 00:10:40.840
very briefly even
briefer than this is,

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:46.050
work with Mani Kotaru,

00:10:46.051 --> 00:10:49.150
who's Sachin's student as I said.

00:10:49.151 --> 00:10:51.395
Sachin and his students
have had a theme

00:10:51.396 --> 00:10:55.035
of using Wi-Fi signals
for various things,

00:10:55.036 --> 00:10:57.490
in this case is localization.

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:00.770
There's a VR headset
that's moving,

00:11:00.771 --> 00:11:02.885
Wi-Fi signals are captured.

00:11:02.886 --> 00:11:05.600
The goal is to localize
based on the signals.

00:11:05.601 --> 00:11:07.990
There's a well-known
mid-seventies algorithm

00:11:07.991 --> 00:11:10.430
called Music that does it.

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:15.595
It cancels the indirect path,
etcetera, it does.

00:11:15.596 --> 00:11:18.385
But it had a 60-centimeter error,

00:11:18.386 --> 00:11:21.900
when music does the localization.

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:24.160
In work that took money more

00:11:24.161 --> 00:11:25.260
than a year and a
half because you

00:11:25.261 --> 00:11:27.680
really it's not like
off the shelf ML,

00:11:27.681 --> 00:11:29.640
it's like you have
work, wrestled with it.

00:11:29.641 --> 00:11:31.070
I actually thought he
won't even succeed,

00:11:31.071 --> 00:11:32.230
maybe there's not
an easy function to

00:11:32.231 --> 00:11:35.005
approximate, but he did.

00:11:35.006 --> 00:11:38.380
There's a submission
on this it's,

00:11:38.381 --> 00:11:41.310
four-centimetre error and it's

00:11:41.311 --> 00:11:44.050
also really fast by
comparison with Music.

00:11:44.051 --> 00:11:47.080
So, this example
is second example,

00:11:47.081 --> 00:11:48.540
and the last one is just,

00:11:48.541 --> 00:11:50.100
I'm going to- I really

00:11:50.101 --> 00:11:51.800
encourage you to read
it because I was quite

00:11:51.801 --> 00:11:56.140
impressed with the way the
physics folks are doing it.

00:11:56.141 --> 00:11:58.600
This is from CERN.

00:11:58.601 --> 00:12:02.980
I'm happy to share this with
folks who beyond the URL.

00:12:02.981 --> 00:12:06.600
So basically, what happens in
the Large Hadron Collider,

00:12:06.601 --> 00:12:08.610
it has got some
elementary particles,

00:12:08.611 --> 00:12:09.860
something is held over here

00:12:09.861 --> 00:12:11.290
and something really fast comes

00:12:11.291 --> 00:12:13.945
and bangs it and then
stuff like muons,

00:12:13.946 --> 00:12:16.065
gluons, all these guys come out,

00:12:16.066 --> 00:12:17.490
and then they traced
the trajectory of

00:12:17.491 --> 00:12:19.485
these particles,
that's their business.

00:12:19.486 --> 00:12:20.990
But the data generated is so

00:12:20.991 --> 00:12:22.435
huge that they had to sensor it,

00:12:22.436 --> 00:12:24.105
they can't store everything.

00:12:24.106 --> 00:12:27.320
The article starts by
saying, in the 60's,

00:12:31.630 --> 00:12:37.650
people used to pore over
these images and reconstruct

00:12:37.651 --> 00:12:38.880
the trajectory is to
find out if they're

00:12:38.881 --> 00:12:40.150
found a new elementary
particle and

00:12:40.151 --> 00:12:43.385
modeling the Higgs Boson type
things showing up or not.

00:12:43.386 --> 00:12:47.390
Then the learning revolution
has just begun and

00:12:47.391 --> 00:12:48.510
it was based on

00:12:48.511 --> 00:12:51.325
grid computing that was
introduced in the 2000's.

00:12:51.326 --> 00:12:53.325
What they're now doing is just,

00:12:53.326 --> 00:12:54.520
it's exactly reconstruction.

00:12:54.521 --> 00:12:56.880
So, my problem of the inverse,

00:12:56.881 --> 00:12:58.680
given the overall
delay find me the

00:12:58.681 --> 00:13:00.770
queue sizes that's called
an inverse problem,

00:13:00.771 --> 00:13:03.460
that is a forward equation
is D equals AQ plus N,

00:13:03.461 --> 00:13:06.170
the inverse equation
is given D find NQ.

00:13:06.171 --> 00:13:07.750
There's another inverse problem,

00:13:07.751 --> 00:13:09.555
inverse problems are in MRI.

00:13:09.556 --> 00:13:11.315
All of those are inverse problems

00:13:11.316 --> 00:13:13.400
and there's another
inverse problem and

00:13:13.401 --> 00:13:15.670
the most recent work
has been about training

00:13:15.671 --> 00:13:17.430
this neural networks to just

00:13:17.431 --> 00:13:19.765
do the inverse problems
and find the trajectories.

00:13:19.766 --> 00:13:22.000
So, what the equivalent
of LASSO for them is

00:13:22.001 --> 00:13:24.420
the classical quantum
mechanics laws

00:13:24.421 --> 00:13:25.825
and then they restrain them.

00:13:25.826 --> 00:13:29.950
I think it's harder acceleration

00:13:29.951 --> 00:13:31.940
plus all that is
beneficially used in

00:13:31.941 --> 00:13:35.360
this case as well. So,
with that I'll stop.

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:42.120
&gt;&gt; The format we're
hoping is that,

00:13:42.121 --> 00:13:44.560
if there are any burning
questions one or two we

00:13:44.561 --> 00:13:45.640
could bring it up now but if

00:13:45.641 --> 00:13:46.900
you have more broader questions,

00:13:46.901 --> 00:13:50.145
we ask that you keep it
for the panel at the end.

00:13:50.146 --> 00:13:58.860
Any quick questions while
Bruce gets setup. Okay.

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:08.685
We'll try my laptop.

00:14:08.686 --> 00:14:10.630
Yes, while Bruce get set up.

00:14:10.631 --> 00:14:13.720
Bruce is from Duke University.

00:14:13.721 --> 00:14:15.220
His interests are in

00:14:15.221 --> 00:14:18.285
large-scale networks as
well as his research.

00:14:18.286 --> 00:14:21.850
Prior to that Bruce was at

00:14:21.851 --> 00:14:25.330
Carnegie Mellon University
where he helped launch

00:14:25.331 --> 00:14:28.095
the Akamai CDN Service

00:14:28.096 --> 00:14:30.480
and he was the first
vice president of research and

00:14:30.481 --> 00:14:32.880
development for Akamai
where he continues to

00:14:32.881 --> 00:14:36.315
hold a role as
vice president for research.

00:14:36.316 --> 00:14:38.040
Bruce's work has won

00:14:38.041 --> 00:14:41.660
the SIGCOMM Networking
Systems Award,

00:14:41.661 --> 00:14:46.400
as well as the IEEE
Cybersecurity Innovation Award.

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:56.690
&gt;&gt; We're going to go with VGA.

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:00.430
&gt;&gt; Got it hooked hooked up there?

00:15:00.431 --> 00:15:01.730
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:13.970
&gt;&gt; There you go.

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:20.045
&gt;&gt; Thank you Ganesh.
It's nice to be here,

00:15:20.046 --> 00:15:22.170
I have to say that I am giving

00:15:22.171 --> 00:15:24.810
this presentation with
some trepidation,

00:15:24.811 --> 00:15:28.605
because I don't really use
ML very much in my work.

00:15:28.606 --> 00:15:31.400
I tried to think of
the best justification

00:15:31.401 --> 00:15:32.710
I could find for myself

00:15:32.711 --> 00:15:34.440
as a speaker and it occurred to

00:15:34.441 --> 00:15:37.410
me that I have ML
in my middle name.

00:15:38.580 --> 00:15:41.960
So, I'm a traditional
systems designer.

00:15:41.961 --> 00:15:46.915
I like to design big systems,

00:15:46.916 --> 00:15:50.730
the biggest example
is the "Akamai CDN",

00:15:50.731 --> 00:15:52.980
large parts of which I designed.

00:15:52.981 --> 00:15:56.210
I like to try to
optimize these systems,

00:15:56.211 --> 00:15:58.770
and I like to fix them.

00:15:58.771 --> 00:16:01.195
One of the things I've discovered

00:16:01.196 --> 00:16:02.850
over and over is that whenever

00:16:02.851 --> 00:16:05.100
you take measurements and

00:16:05.101 --> 00:16:07.230
look at how your
system's performing,

00:16:07.231 --> 00:16:09.855
you find out that it's broken,

00:16:09.856 --> 00:16:13.110
broken either not
functioning correctly,

00:16:13.111 --> 00:16:16.760
or it's just performing
very poorly.

00:16:16.761 --> 00:16:20.220
There's a methodology
that I've used

00:16:20.221 --> 00:16:23.980
many times to try to
either improve systems,

00:16:23.981 --> 00:16:27.075
or design systems with
new capabilities,

00:16:27.076 --> 00:16:31.675
that I call data-driven design
of network systems.

00:16:31.676 --> 00:16:33.480
So, the idea is that,

00:16:33.481 --> 00:16:37.600
you look around for
existing systems that are

00:16:37.601 --> 00:16:42.745
already deployed
on a large scale,

00:16:42.746 --> 00:16:45.180
and from which you can
collect a lot of data,

00:16:45.181 --> 00:16:47.835
possibly data of different types,

00:16:47.836 --> 00:16:49.610
and then you think about,

00:16:49.611 --> 00:16:51.790
how could we marry
these datasets,

00:16:51.791 --> 00:16:55.110
to help us evaluate
new system designs?

00:16:55.111 --> 00:16:57.695
Maybe for a system that
does something different,

00:16:57.696 --> 00:17:01.780
or that does the same thing
but in a very different way.

00:17:01.781 --> 00:17:04.145
So, one example of this,

00:17:04.146 --> 00:17:07.140
maybe the first one I
was involved in was,

00:17:07.141 --> 00:17:10.610
there was a new system
that had been developed at

00:17:10.611 --> 00:17:14.120
Carnegie Mellon around 2000 or

00:17:14.121 --> 00:17:19.710
so for purely
peer-to-peer delivery

00:17:19.711 --> 00:17:24.465
of live or real-time video
called N System Multicast.

00:17:24.466 --> 00:17:27.040
This was not my work,

00:17:27.041 --> 00:17:31.330
it was Wei Jiang, and Saddy Raul,

00:17:31.331 --> 00:17:33.970
and several others, they won

00:17:33.971 --> 00:17:37.035
the Sigmetrics Test of time award

00:17:37.036 --> 00:17:39.705
for their paper describing
N System Multicast.

00:17:39.706 --> 00:17:43.685
But, they didn't have
a very good way of

00:17:43.686 --> 00:17:46.600
evaluating whether or
not this system could

00:17:46.601 --> 00:17:49.490
really hold up under
real workloads.

00:17:49.491 --> 00:17:51.130
So, one of the things we did was,

00:17:51.131 --> 00:17:53.480
we gathered all the logs from

00:17:53.481 --> 00:17:58.650
Akamai real-time
video delivery system,

00:17:58.651 --> 00:18:02.990
then we used that to drive

00:18:02.991 --> 00:18:04.660
simulations of ESM
and the kinds of

00:18:04.661 --> 00:18:07.155
questions we were
interested in were, well,

00:18:07.156 --> 00:18:09.290
when there's
a massive flash crowd at

00:18:09.291 --> 00:18:12.105
the beginning of
some scheduled event,

00:18:12.106 --> 00:18:16.060
and peers are joining
and leaving rapidly,

00:18:16.061 --> 00:18:17.980
is the system stable?

00:18:17.981 --> 00:18:20.030
Most of the time, we
found that in fact,

00:18:20.031 --> 00:18:22.910
even without the
dedicated infrastructure,

00:18:22.911 --> 00:18:24.950
that Akamai had deployed

00:18:24.951 --> 00:18:26.955
for its real-time
streaming network,

00:18:26.956 --> 00:18:34.625
the ESM was still
mostly successful.

00:18:34.626 --> 00:18:38.670
Let me give you another
example of this kind of work.

00:18:38.671 --> 00:18:43.065
So, here's a fact that
maybe people don't know,

00:18:43.066 --> 00:18:47.135
but TLS certificate
revocation checking

00:18:47.136 --> 00:18:50.320
is broken in a bad way,
and here's how.

00:18:50.321 --> 00:18:52.260
Your mobile device doesn't

00:18:52.261 --> 00:18:54.710
check for certificate revocation.

00:18:54.711 --> 00:18:57.540
So, what this means is,

00:18:57.541 --> 00:19:00.680
if you go to

00:19:00.681 --> 00:19:03.210
some website where you
provide your password,

00:19:03.211 --> 00:19:06.550
and maybe use the same password
at a lot of websites,

00:19:06.551 --> 00:19:11.125
and somebody has stolen that
site's private key somehow.

00:19:11.126 --> 00:19:12.175
Maybe through a bug,

00:19:12.176 --> 00:19:13.310
like the Heartbleed bug,

00:19:13.311 --> 00:19:16.025
and somehow they have

00:19:16.026 --> 00:19:19.870
interfered with DNS and you've
gone to an imposter site,

00:19:19.871 --> 00:19:22.630
and despite the fact
that the true owner of

00:19:22.631 --> 00:19:27.680
that certificate has reported
it as no longer valid,

00:19:27.681 --> 00:19:29.730
and put it on a revocation list,

00:19:29.731 --> 00:19:33.195
your device will
not check for that.

00:19:33.196 --> 00:19:34.710
I know this because I've tested

00:19:34.711 --> 00:19:39.690
all the mobile devices,
Android devices, iPhones.

00:19:39.691 --> 00:19:42.180
I couldn't find any example
in which any device

00:19:42.181 --> 00:19:45.140
ever checked for
certificate revocation.

00:19:45.141 --> 00:19:48.825
The reason they don't is
that it's too expensive.

00:19:48.826 --> 00:19:50.460
One of the things
I found a little

00:19:50.461 --> 00:19:52.310
frustrating this morning going to

00:19:52.311 --> 00:19:55.780
talks about formally verifying
security properties,

00:19:55.781 --> 00:19:57.400
making sure your
implementations of

00:19:57.401 --> 00:19:59.140
the protocols are
correct and so on,

00:19:59.141 --> 00:20:01.640
is that there are
these other economic reasons

00:20:01.641 --> 00:20:04.700
why whole parts of the protocol
just aren't implemented.

00:20:04.701 --> 00:20:06.130
So, on mobile devices,

00:20:06.131 --> 00:20:09.585
they don't do certificate
revocation checking because it

00:20:09.586 --> 00:20:11.460
will be too much
bandwidth to download

00:20:11.461 --> 00:20:13.465
the whole list of
revoked certificates.

00:20:13.466 --> 00:20:15.910
Or, it would take
too long to contact

00:20:15.911 --> 00:20:19.610
an OCSP server to ask is
this certificate valid.

00:20:19.611 --> 00:20:22.780
Or, there's new protocols
like OCSP stapling,

00:20:22.781 --> 00:20:24.560
but only two or three percent of

00:20:24.561 --> 00:20:26.680
servers are supporting that.

00:20:26.681 --> 00:20:29.585
So, It's just not done,

00:20:29.586 --> 00:20:32.335
and this is a crying shame.

00:20:32.336 --> 00:20:35.000
So, we started thinking about,

00:20:35.001 --> 00:20:36.830
is there data out
there that would make

00:20:36.831 --> 00:20:39.055
it possible to
solve this problem?

00:20:39.056 --> 00:20:41.290
It is not difficult to find

00:20:41.291 --> 00:20:43.820
the list of all the
revoked certificates,

00:20:43.821 --> 00:20:46.340
because they're on
the certificate revocation list.

00:20:46.341 --> 00:20:47.980
But what's harder to find is,

00:20:47.981 --> 00:20:51.885
a list of all the valid
certificates, until recently,

00:20:51.886 --> 00:20:55.620
when Google was so annoyed

00:20:55.621 --> 00:21:00.555
that semantic had made
some fake certificates,

00:21:00.556 --> 00:21:02.640
that they launched something

00:21:02.641 --> 00:21:04.495
called certificate transparency,

00:21:04.496 --> 00:21:06.945
and now, they

00:21:06.946 --> 00:21:10.095
basically pressuring
certificate authorities,

00:21:10.096 --> 00:21:13.720
to put into, just to
spice up my talk,

00:21:13.721 --> 00:21:18.450
I'm going to say
a blockchain type of log,

00:21:18.451 --> 00:21:21.030
every certificate
that they issue.

00:21:21.031 --> 00:21:23.360
In addition to that, there's
some research projects

00:21:23.361 --> 00:21:24.540
that have been going around,

00:21:24.541 --> 00:21:27.980
scanning the whole
IPv4 address space,

00:21:27.981 --> 00:21:31.495
pulling every certificate
they can from port 443.

00:21:31.496 --> 00:21:33.080
So, suddenly we have

00:21:33.081 --> 00:21:35.160
a new dataset we didn't
have before which is,

00:21:35.161 --> 00:21:36.645
all the valid certificates.

00:21:36.646 --> 00:21:38.910
So, we had the invalid
ones before,

00:21:38.911 --> 00:21:41.690
and now we have the valid ones.

00:21:42.080 --> 00:21:44.655
By the way, it's a lot
of certificates.

00:21:44.656 --> 00:21:47.195
We're talking about
tens of millions.

00:21:47.196 --> 00:21:49.420
This suggests a way

00:21:49.421 --> 00:21:54.080
to represent in
a very compact data structure,

00:21:54.081 --> 00:21:57.265
complete information
about every certificate

00:21:57.266 --> 00:22:00.285
indicating whether it's
been revoked or not.

00:22:00.286 --> 00:22:03.015
The structure is called
a "Filter Cascade".

00:22:03.016 --> 00:22:05.290
So, the idea is that,

00:22:05.291 --> 00:22:07.745
you take all the
revoked certificates,

00:22:07.746 --> 00:22:10.460
and you insert them
into a "Bloom Filter".

00:22:10.461 --> 00:22:13.280
Now, that would be
fine as a solution

00:22:13.281 --> 00:22:14.350
except that it'd be really

00:22:14.351 --> 00:22:16.350
unfair if there was
a false positive,

00:22:16.351 --> 00:22:19.990
and some websites certificate
is now marked as

00:22:19.991 --> 00:22:24.170
revoked because there was
just a bad collision.

00:22:24.171 --> 00:22:28.329
So, then you take all the
non-revoked certificates,

00:22:28.330 --> 00:22:34.155
and you ask, "Are there
any false positives?"

00:22:34.156 --> 00:22:36.140
For all the false positives,

00:22:36.141 --> 00:22:38.290
you create a "Bloom
Filter" to hold those.

00:22:38.291 --> 00:22:41.510
There are a lot more non
revoked certificates

00:22:41.511 --> 00:22:42.555
than revoked.

00:22:42.556 --> 00:22:44.810
So, that it's important
that the first

00:22:44.811 --> 00:22:48.640
level you insert
the revoked ones,

00:22:48.641 --> 00:22:50.020
so you can have a small table,

00:22:50.021 --> 00:22:52.495
but with a really small
false positive rate.

00:22:52.496 --> 00:22:55.520
Then you say, "What about

00:22:55.521 --> 00:22:57.840
revoked certificates
that accidentally

00:22:57.841 --> 00:22:59.210
get marked as false positives,

00:22:59.211 --> 00:23:00.430
meaning they would
be considered as

00:23:00.431 --> 00:23:02.475
non revoked you
make it a little?"

00:23:02.476 --> 00:23:05.945
So, you get this
geometric decrease,

00:23:05.946 --> 00:23:10.130
and it turns out that
this "Filter Cascade",

00:23:10.131 --> 00:23:12.135
it has 43 levels,

00:23:12.136 --> 00:23:14.440
but you only expect to do

00:23:14.441 --> 00:23:17.700
ten hashes to look up
the status of any certificate,

00:23:17.701 --> 00:23:19.300
and the whole thing,

00:23:19.301 --> 00:23:22.170
only takes 1.1 megabytes to

00:23:22.171 --> 00:23:26.535
represent the precise status
of every certificate.

00:23:26.536 --> 00:23:28.730
There's a lower bound,

00:23:28.731 --> 00:23:31.930
an information theoretic lower
bound of 0.77 megabytes,

00:23:31.931 --> 00:23:33.440
so you're not going
to do much better.

00:23:33.441 --> 00:23:37.205
So basically,

00:23:37.206 --> 00:23:41.150
today there are
60 million valid certificates,

00:23:41.151 --> 00:23:43.560
that number is
about 40 million higher

00:23:43.561 --> 00:23:44.635
than it was two years ago.

00:23:44.636 --> 00:23:47.865
Maybe 50 million, because
of Let's Encrypt.

00:23:47.866 --> 00:23:50.155
Today, in September we had

00:23:50.156 --> 00:23:52.890
850 thousand revoked
certificates,

00:23:52.891 --> 00:23:58.125
and so that would take 1.1
megabytes, but actually,

00:23:58.126 --> 00:23:59.780
the list changes very slowly,

00:23:59.781 --> 00:24:01.755
so you can just publish diffs,

00:24:01.756 --> 00:24:06.165
and the whole thing is
only 40 kilobytes per day.

00:24:06.166 --> 00:24:10.665
So, this is an example of
data-driven system design,

00:24:10.666 --> 00:24:14.215
what's out there, how
can we exploit it.

00:24:14.216 --> 00:24:17.570
Now, I want to say a little bit
about Machine Learning.

00:24:17.571 --> 00:24:21.110
The application of
Machine Learning

00:24:21.111 --> 00:24:23.730
in computer networking is old.

00:24:23.731 --> 00:24:27.830
So, here's a paper from
a survey from 2008 that's

00:24:27.831 --> 00:24:32.470
been cited a thousand times,
Traffic classification.

00:24:32.471 --> 00:24:34.910
This is a thing people
have done for a long time.

00:24:34.911 --> 00:24:37.685
Trying to figure out,
is this an attack?

00:24:37.686 --> 00:24:40.290
What kind of application
is running here?

00:24:40.291 --> 00:24:42.160
This is an old application.

00:24:42.161 --> 00:24:47.080
I was thinking a little bit
about places where you

00:24:47.081 --> 00:24:49.170
might use Machine Learning in

00:24:49.171 --> 00:24:52.095
the context of
content delivery networks.

00:24:52.096 --> 00:24:55.760
So, one of the things
that Akamai tries to do,

00:24:55.761 --> 00:24:58.875
is predict for each client
on the internet,

00:24:58.876 --> 00:25:00.770
a billion clients,

00:25:00.771 --> 00:25:03.695
which of our servers would
give best performance.

00:25:03.696 --> 00:25:06.010
This is an example where you

00:25:06.011 --> 00:25:08.810
have multiple datasets
of different character,

00:25:08.811 --> 00:25:10.135
you have past performance,

00:25:10.136 --> 00:25:11.560
you have network measurements,

00:25:11.561 --> 00:25:13.595
you have network
topology information,

00:25:13.596 --> 00:25:15.265
you're trying to
make a prediction,

00:25:15.266 --> 00:25:18.495
and here you could try
to use Machine Learning.

00:25:18.496 --> 00:25:21.265
Another application is
trying to figure out,

00:25:21.266 --> 00:25:23.130
is this a legitimate client?

00:25:23.131 --> 00:25:25.140
We have a lot of
trouble with botnets

00:25:25.141 --> 00:25:27.575
trying to break into
banking customers.

00:25:27.576 --> 00:25:31.335
So, we want to develop
a reputation for a client,

00:25:31.336 --> 00:25:34.170
or estimate the physical
location of a client.

00:25:34.171 --> 00:25:37.050
This is easy if
there's some way for

00:25:37.051 --> 00:25:38.760
a client to click on
the "Allow" button

00:25:38.761 --> 00:25:40.260
to give up their location?

00:25:40.261 --> 00:25:42.030
But Akamai doesn't
touch clients the

00:25:42.031 --> 00:25:45.120
same way that say
a content provider does.

00:25:45.530 --> 00:25:48.710
These kinds of things work best

00:25:48.711 --> 00:25:53.005
when the penalty for
false positives is low.

00:25:53.006 --> 00:25:55.220
So, it's okay if whatever

00:25:55.221 --> 00:25:58.515
algorithm you're
using does poorly,

00:25:58.516 --> 00:26:00.880
as long as it doesn't

00:26:00.881 --> 00:26:03.140
hurt that much when you
make the wrong decision.

00:26:03.141 --> 00:26:06.080
So, when we decide
a client is suspicious,

00:26:06.081 --> 00:26:07.835
we don't ban it, but
we rate limit it.

00:26:07.836 --> 00:26:09.970
Or, if we decided a participant

00:26:09.971 --> 00:26:12.480
in our peer to peer
content delivery system,

00:26:12.481 --> 00:26:15.110
Akamai has tens of
millions of clients in

