&gt;&gt; Great thanks guys.

00:28:34.001 --> 00:28:36.080
So, I'm here talking

00:28:36.081 --> 00:28:38.350
about the work of the Azure
Physical Networking Team.

00:28:38.351 --> 00:28:39.890
We're called the
Physical Networking Team

00:28:39.891 --> 00:28:41.710
because you might
imagine we're in

00:28:41.711 --> 00:28:43.060
charge of actually
getting packets between

00:28:43.061 --> 00:28:46.180
all the servers inside
of our data centers.

00:28:46.181 --> 00:28:48.310
I want to talk to you
a little bit about

00:28:48.311 --> 00:28:50.125
the environment in which we work.

00:28:50.126 --> 00:28:52.515
So, we're building
a really large network.

00:28:52.516 --> 00:28:54.860
Hundreds of thousands links
of each data center,

00:28:54.861 --> 00:28:57.365
hundreds of data centers
around the world.

00:28:57.366 --> 00:29:01.785
You know when you talk about
large scale large data sets,

00:29:01.786 --> 00:29:02.920
the network is very good at

00:29:02.921 --> 00:29:04.510
generating those kinds of things.

00:29:04.511 --> 00:29:06.835
And just to be clear,

00:29:06.836 --> 00:29:10.130
the number one job for
my team is high availability.

00:29:10.131 --> 00:29:11.820
Is making sure that
every packet that gets

00:29:11.821 --> 00:29:13.890
sent by every server is
running in our network

00:29:13.891 --> 00:29:16.010
actually makes it
across that network and

00:29:16.011 --> 00:29:18.825
received appropriately by
the destination servers.

00:29:18.826 --> 00:29:21.770
And the challenge we have is
that at this large scale,

00:29:21.771 --> 00:29:24.465
the law large numbers
is not your friend.

00:29:24.466 --> 00:29:26.985
Yes we see lots of
nice median behavior,

00:29:26.986 --> 00:29:28.690
but it turns out
nobody cares about

00:29:28.691 --> 00:29:30.495
what happens at
the 50th percentile.

00:29:30.496 --> 00:29:32.370
People who care about
what happened at

00:29:32.371 --> 00:29:34.350
the strange edge conditions,

00:29:34.351 --> 00:29:36.890
what is the very high
nine's percentile

00:29:36.891 --> 00:29:39.630
of your behavior of packet
loss and everything else?

00:29:39.631 --> 00:29:41.450
So, instead of Occam's Razor,

00:29:41.451 --> 00:29:43.915
where the simplest explanation
is the most likely,

00:29:43.916 --> 00:29:45.270
you get Murphy's Law.

00:29:45.271 --> 00:29:46.670
There is something crazy

00:29:46.671 --> 00:29:48.060
happening in some
part of the network.

00:29:48.061 --> 00:29:50.935
There's going to generate
really weird behavior and

00:29:50.936 --> 00:29:52.650
my team's job is to go

00:29:52.651 --> 00:29:54.650
figure out what that
is and stop it.

00:29:54.651 --> 00:29:58.910
So, we take a network this

00:29:58.911 --> 00:30:00.990
is actually sort of a shot
of a rendering of

00:30:00.991 --> 00:30:03.360
one small part of our network
in one small data center,

00:30:03.361 --> 00:30:04.930
you can see all the
inter connectivity

00:30:04.931 --> 00:30:08.495
and go find that proverbial
needle in a haystack.

00:30:08.496 --> 00:30:10.600
Sadly we're actually
pretty good at it,

00:30:10.601 --> 00:30:13.620
but sadly it does often take
us quite a bit of time.

00:30:13.621 --> 00:30:16.040
Again the time to mitigate
these problems is also

00:30:16.041 --> 00:30:18.430
very important because
our customers are suffering.

00:30:18.431 --> 00:30:21.360
They are bleeding
money reputation and

00:30:21.361 --> 00:30:23.060
everything while we go and

00:30:23.061 --> 00:30:25.645
try and find these
problems and resolve them.

00:30:25.646 --> 00:30:28.855
So, how can machine
learning help us with this?

00:30:28.856 --> 00:30:31.940
So, I'm going to have
sort of a mixed message.

00:30:31.941 --> 00:30:33.140
I'm going to show you a couple of

00:30:33.141 --> 00:30:34.360
problems where I
think ML probably

00:30:34.361 --> 00:30:35.620
has some things to offer

00:30:35.621 --> 00:30:37.040
us and we've been
getting some benefits.

00:30:37.041 --> 00:30:38.930
I'm not trying to characterize
some things where

00:30:38.931 --> 00:30:41.040
ML is probably not
the best approach

00:30:41.041 --> 00:30:43.220
and I hope I have enough time
to end with a little bit of

00:30:43.221 --> 00:30:46.160
commentary about from
a practitioner's guide,

00:30:46.161 --> 00:30:47.920
if you're a normal person
and want to work with

00:30:47.921 --> 00:30:49.810
system scientists or
research scientists,

00:30:49.811 --> 00:30:51.150
you want to work with ML people,

00:30:51.151 --> 00:30:54.520
how can you cross the knowledge
gap between those two?

00:30:54.521 --> 00:30:56.770
I'll talk about sort of the three

00:30:56.771 --> 00:30:58.340
examples and I'll come back to

00:30:58.341 --> 00:31:02.115
sort the commentary text on
the side as we go through.

00:31:02.116 --> 00:31:03.670
Let me start with
a problem called

00:31:03.671 --> 00:31:05.805
Layer One Topology Generation.

00:31:05.806 --> 00:31:07.670
So, the basic problem

00:31:07.671 --> 00:31:11.140
here is we want to keep
our network from failing.

00:31:11.141 --> 00:31:13.050
We want to keeps
our optical network from failing

00:31:13.051 --> 00:31:15.440
from various data centers
or regions that are

00:31:15.441 --> 00:31:17.445
network being partition from
the rest of the network

00:31:17.446 --> 00:31:20.000
due to fiber cuts
rather fiber failures.

00:31:20.001 --> 00:31:22.810
Now, you're like, hey guys
this is an easy problem.

00:31:22.811 --> 00:31:24.385
You tell me the availability

00:31:24.386 --> 00:31:27.480
distributions time between
failure time to repair

00:31:27.481 --> 00:31:30.540
for your fiber and we can
predict what the availability

00:31:30.541 --> 00:31:34.215
what any particular
region should be.

00:31:34.216 --> 00:31:35.895
The problem is the following.

00:31:35.896 --> 00:31:37.860
Fiber providers( a) don't

00:31:37.861 --> 00:31:40.050
really know this information
and (b) innovative they

00:31:40.051 --> 00:31:41.645
did they're going to lie

00:31:41.646 --> 00:31:43.390
and they're not really
going to give us someone

00:31:43.391 --> 00:31:45.260
who's trying to
acquire fiber to build

00:31:45.261 --> 00:31:47.655
our network accurate information.

00:31:47.656 --> 00:31:49.890
So the question is, given

00:31:49.891 --> 00:31:51.420
the fact that we have to acquire

00:31:51.421 --> 00:31:53.270
new fiber paths all the time to

00:31:53.271 --> 00:31:56.185
continue to build and
extend our network,

00:31:56.186 --> 00:31:58.390
we need to acquire
enough fiber paths in

00:31:58.391 --> 00:31:59.820
the right kinds of fiber paths to

00:31:59.821 --> 00:32:01.725
the availability
targets that we want,

00:32:01.726 --> 00:32:05.470
how can we actually predict
what this time between

00:32:05.471 --> 00:32:07.250
failure and time to repair
distributions are going to

00:32:07.251 --> 00:32:09.295
be for a new fiber path?

00:32:09.296 --> 00:32:11.280
Now, we have a large bunch

00:32:11.281 --> 00:32:13.385
of fiber pass already
in our inventory.

00:32:13.386 --> 00:32:15.590
So, if we could take

00:32:15.591 --> 00:32:18.290
our experience that we've
actually measured from

00:32:18.291 --> 00:32:20.410
existing fiber paths
and figure out how to

00:32:20.411 --> 00:32:23.395
apply that to estimate the
characteristics for new fiber,

00:32:23.396 --> 00:32:25.190
we could do a pretty good job.

00:32:25.191 --> 00:32:27.350
Challenge here is
there are lots and

00:32:27.351 --> 00:32:29.360
lots and lots of
relevant dimensions.

00:32:29.361 --> 00:32:30.790
There's where the fiber goes,

00:32:30.791 --> 00:32:32.710
there's how many construction
sites it goes through,

00:32:32.711 --> 00:32:34.460
there's how many people
live along that fiber,

00:32:34.461 --> 00:32:36.320
there's how many kilometers
are aerial fiber,

00:32:36.321 --> 00:32:37.840
how many kilometers
are buried fiber.

00:32:37.841 --> 00:32:39.675
Lots and lots of other variables

00:32:39.676 --> 00:32:41.950
that can be extracted
for each of these paths.

00:32:41.951 --> 00:32:45.205
Unfortunately, we don't
want topology decisions

00:32:45.206 --> 00:32:46.770
or fiber path selection decisions

00:32:46.771 --> 00:32:48.410
to be made based
on human emotion.

00:32:48.411 --> 00:32:50.120
There's oh, we bought
some fiber from

00:32:50.121 --> 00:32:52.450
this company last time and
they really were nice to us.

00:32:52.451 --> 00:32:53.760
We like them so we
should buy more fiber

00:32:53.761 --> 00:32:54.880
from them or I know

00:32:54.881 --> 00:32:56.360
this person who worked
on that project

00:32:56.361 --> 00:32:57.995
and they're very
reputable so let's buy.

00:32:57.996 --> 00:33:01.205
Right? That's not the right
approach to this.

00:33:01.206 --> 00:33:03.740
So, we've actually had

00:33:03.741 --> 00:33:05.280
some pretty good initial results

00:33:05.281 --> 00:33:07.200
in sort of this
overall problem space.

00:33:07.201 --> 00:33:07.950
This is data from

00:33:07.951 --> 00:33:09.610
not exactly the problem
I just described,

00:33:09.611 --> 00:33:11.750
but a very related
one of trying to

00:33:11.751 --> 00:33:14.920
estimate what the availability
of particular portions

00:33:14.921 --> 00:33:17.100
of our topology is
going to be based

00:33:17.101 --> 00:33:20.710
on our estimated and
then simulated data,

00:33:20.711 --> 00:33:22.975
versus the actual availability.

00:33:22.976 --> 00:33:24.440
So, the green bars

00:33:24.441 --> 00:33:26.485
represent our actual
observed availability,

00:33:26.486 --> 00:33:28.150
the blue is the average of

00:33:28.151 --> 00:33:30.910
our simulated an estimated
availability and you

00:33:30.911 --> 00:33:31.980
can see in some cases we

00:33:31.981 --> 00:33:33.920
undershoot in some
cases we overshoot.

00:33:33.921 --> 00:33:36.275
We're doing a lot better
than random chance here.

00:33:36.276 --> 00:33:38.570
So, again that gives us hope
that this is the kind of

00:33:38.571 --> 00:33:40.750
thing where classification
algorithms can

00:33:40.751 --> 00:33:43.310
extract dimensionality
that's not obvious to

00:33:43.311 --> 00:33:44.845
a human being that is still

00:33:44.846 --> 00:33:46.465
going to give us
good business value.

00:33:46.466 --> 00:33:48.810
Is this ML, is a statistics,

00:33:48.811 --> 00:33:50.240
should we be using PCA or

00:33:50.241 --> 00:33:51.730
other kinds of
straight stats versus

00:33:51.731 --> 00:33:56.245
more harder to explain
classification algorithms?

00:33:56.246 --> 00:33:57.990
That's really interesting
conversation to have

00:33:57.991 --> 00:34:00.330
me we'll talk about
during the panel.

00:34:00.331 --> 00:34:05.000
Another problem, optical
performance optimization.

00:34:05.001 --> 00:34:08.880
So, we have what we
call Open Line Systems.

00:34:08.881 --> 00:34:09.950
Microsoft has
actually been one of

00:34:09.951 --> 00:34:12.335
the companies out there
driving towards this.

00:34:12.336 --> 00:34:15.340
Rather than buying a highly
integrated system from one of

00:34:15.341 --> 00:34:16.810
the standard optical
vendors where

00:34:16.811 --> 00:34:18.540
you have to buy all the
equipment from them,

00:34:18.541 --> 00:34:21.100
we're trying to get to
a desegregated ecosystem

00:34:21.101 --> 00:34:22.680
where you can buy line systems

00:34:22.681 --> 00:34:24.680
and amplifiers from
one company transponders from

00:34:24.681 --> 00:34:26.960
another and mix it
or match together.

00:34:26.961 --> 00:34:29.050
One of the reasons is
because these systems,

00:34:29.051 --> 00:34:30.735
when you open them up,

00:34:30.736 --> 00:34:32.650
actually have lots
of parameters that

00:34:32.651 --> 00:34:34.590
we can tune to maximize

00:34:34.591 --> 00:34:36.650
the number of bits per
second that can actually be

00:34:36.651 --> 00:34:38.990
delivered across
any individual piece of glass.

00:34:38.991 --> 00:34:40.490
These are things
like the transmit

00:34:40.491 --> 00:34:41.790
power, the received power,

00:34:41.791 --> 00:34:44.510
how many amplifiers you
put along your path,

00:34:44.511 --> 00:34:46.140
all of this kind of stuff.

00:34:46.141 --> 00:34:48.615
Is this a good machine
learning problem?

00:34:48.616 --> 00:34:49.900
Okay, we've got a bunch of

00:34:49.901 --> 00:34:51.700
parameters and we want to
set them appropriately.

00:34:51.701 --> 00:34:53.170
This is a control system,

00:34:53.171 --> 00:34:55.750
a problem in any one's sense.

00:34:55.751 --> 00:34:58.100
I'm going to argue,
no. This isn't.

00:34:58.101 --> 00:35:01.190
Why? Availabilities is are
job number one, right?

00:35:01.191 --> 00:35:02.800
So, I can actually tolerate

00:35:02.801 --> 00:35:04.440
many of the errors that a
machine learning system

00:35:04.441 --> 00:35:06.210
might put into
the misclassification

00:35:06.211 --> 00:35:08.290
or misestimation
of the parameters.

00:35:08.291 --> 00:35:11.060
Two, I have a really good set.

00:35:11.061 --> 00:35:13.080
I have a physics textbook
that more or less

00:35:13.081 --> 00:35:15.970
describes exactly the
plant model of how

00:35:15.971 --> 00:35:18.570
this optical line system
and all the amplifiers in

00:35:18.571 --> 00:35:19.950
power generation and

00:35:19.951 --> 00:35:22.530
noise perturbations
is going to work.

00:35:22.531 --> 00:35:25.835
So, I can use much more
standard control theory,

00:35:25.836 --> 00:35:28.220
because I have that forward
plant model to say,

00:35:28.221 --> 00:35:29.730
"If I use this set of parameters,

00:35:29.731 --> 00:35:31.100
what's actually going to happen,

00:35:31.101 --> 00:35:32.340
what's going to
happen my signal?"

00:35:32.341 --> 00:35:35.140
And I can then spin that loop
as often and only when

00:35:35.141 --> 00:35:38.515
needed to try and tune
the system, okay?

00:35:38.516 --> 00:35:40.040
If you've got to something where

00:35:40.041 --> 00:35:42.295
the physical layer
is well understood,

00:35:42.296 --> 00:35:43.800
I don't quite see
what we're going to

00:35:43.801 --> 00:35:44.930
use ML to try and do this,

00:35:44.931 --> 00:35:46.040
even though I do see a lot of

00:35:46.041 --> 00:35:47.220
people out there trying to use

00:35:47.221 --> 00:35:51.000
ML kinds of approaches to
this sort of a problem.

00:35:51.001 --> 00:35:54.485
Third problem,
network availability.

00:35:54.486 --> 00:35:58.090
Hopefully, by now, you can
see where the thinking is.

00:35:58.091 --> 00:36:01.175
Essentially, if you've
got a really big dataset,

00:36:01.176 --> 00:36:03.670
you've got the potential for
machine learning problem.

00:36:03.671 --> 00:36:06.200
If what's underlying
that big dataset is

00:36:06.201 --> 00:36:07.440
a bunch of physical layers or

00:36:07.441 --> 00:36:08.840
properties that are
well understood,

00:36:08.841 --> 00:36:09.990
you understand the interactions

00:36:09.991 --> 00:36:11.125
between all the variables,

00:36:11.126 --> 00:36:12.440
you're probably better off

00:36:12.441 --> 00:36:13.890
using standard control theory and

00:36:13.891 --> 00:36:17.355
some forward plant model
to drive your system.

00:36:17.356 --> 00:36:20.020
If you're in a world
where you don't even know

00:36:20.021 --> 00:36:21.310
which of these variables
are actually

00:36:21.311 --> 00:36:22.655
salient and most important,

00:36:22.656 --> 00:36:24.840
you don't know which
ones are going to be

00:36:24.841 --> 00:36:27.585
most descriptive in
predicting the outcome,

00:36:27.586 --> 00:36:29.560
that's where a bunch

00:36:29.561 --> 00:36:31.410
of machine learning types
of algorithms can come

00:36:31.411 --> 00:36:33.450
up and actually help the surface

00:36:33.451 --> 00:36:35.230
a much better model than

00:36:35.231 --> 00:36:37.465
any human being is
going to come up with.

00:36:37.466 --> 00:36:40.620
Let's look at this last one,
network availability.

00:36:40.621 --> 00:36:44.555
So, again, let's talk
about preventing failures.

00:36:44.556 --> 00:36:46.100
Now, it turns out
when you have a big

00:36:46.101 --> 00:36:47.435
complicated network like this,

00:36:47.436 --> 00:36:49.235
I have lots of redundant paths.

00:36:49.236 --> 00:36:51.300
If any device or a link fails and

00:36:51.301 --> 00:36:53.980
actually shuts itself
off when it fails,

00:36:53.981 --> 00:36:55.905
I am totally fine.

00:36:55.906 --> 00:36:58.560
The problem we have is what
we call Gray switch failures,

00:36:58.561 --> 00:37:00.920
where some piece of
equipment actually starts

00:37:00.921 --> 00:37:02.540
dropping some small fraction

00:37:02.541 --> 00:37:03.905
of the packets that
go through it,

00:37:03.906 --> 00:37:05.740
but not enough that
it actually fail to

00:37:05.741 --> 00:37:07.915
stops and takes itself
out of service.

00:37:07.916 --> 00:37:10.915
When it stays in service
while dropping packets,

00:37:10.916 --> 00:37:12.920
now that's a problem
my team has to go

00:37:12.921 --> 00:37:15.630
through and find the device
and shut it down,

00:37:15.631 --> 00:37:16.930
and there's going
to be customer pain

00:37:16.931 --> 00:37:18.485
and impact until we do so.

00:37:18.486 --> 00:37:21.495
This really is the needle
in a haystack problem.

00:37:21.496 --> 00:37:24.440
Again, we have lots of
different data sources.

00:37:24.441 --> 00:37:26.010
In part, because
we've tried many,

00:37:26.011 --> 00:37:27.995
many different approaches
to this problem.

00:37:27.996 --> 00:37:30.280
So, for example, we've
set a Pingmeshes.

00:37:30.281 --> 00:37:32.460
We have all of our servers
sending ping packets through

00:37:32.461 --> 00:37:33.535
the network and we're measuring

00:37:33.536 --> 00:37:35.140
latency and failure rates.

00:37:35.141 --> 00:37:37.300
We have targeted probe systems,

00:37:37.301 --> 00:37:39.710
which are actually sending
different types of

00:37:39.711 --> 00:37:41.500
probe packets of
different kinds to try

00:37:41.501 --> 00:37:43.775
and simulate different behaviors,

00:37:43.776 --> 00:37:45.980
and we can target more probes
on the links that we

00:37:45.981 --> 00:37:48.510
think might be dropping packets,

00:37:48.511 --> 00:37:50.210
we've got error messages,

00:37:50.211 --> 00:37:52.380
basically debugging pirnits
that are coming back from

00:37:52.381 --> 00:37:53.990
the switches that may or may not

00:37:53.991 --> 00:37:56.110
indicate a problem on
the switches themselves.

00:37:56.111 --> 00:37:58.420
We've got these end-to-end
service health metrics,

00:37:58.421 --> 00:37:59.410
all the services running

00:37:59.411 --> 00:38:00.760
across the network
which are telling us,

00:38:00.761 --> 00:38:02.040
"I'm happy, I'm not happy,

00:38:02.041 --> 00:38:04.365
I'm getting worse,
I'm getting better."

00:38:04.366 --> 00:38:08.220
Essentially, the problem
we now have is,

00:38:08.221 --> 00:38:10.070
how do we combine
all these things

00:38:10.071 --> 00:38:11.655
to localize the problem?

00:38:11.656 --> 00:38:14.450
We have a whole bunch
of noise coming out of

00:38:14.451 --> 00:38:16.680
the best classification
and recognition systems

00:38:16.681 --> 00:38:18.665
that we were able to
build individually.

00:38:18.666 --> 00:38:21.190
So, this is now one of
those metal level things.

00:38:21.191 --> 00:38:24.000
One set of technology for
this would be boosting.

00:38:24.001 --> 00:38:27.760
How can we use boosting is
a way to pull more signal out

00:38:27.761 --> 00:38:29.300
of the noise being
generated by each of

00:38:29.301 --> 00:38:31.445
these other smaller
classification systems.

00:38:31.446 --> 00:38:34.040
We've also use
stochastic gradient descent

00:38:34.041 --> 00:38:35.615
and other kinds of algorithms,

00:38:35.616 --> 00:38:37.220
in a fault propagation model,

00:38:37.221 --> 00:38:39.060
to try and work
backwards to what is

00:38:39.061 --> 00:38:40.845
the most likely explanation

00:38:40.846 --> 00:38:43.305
for this set of symptoms that
we're currently observing.

00:38:43.306 --> 00:38:45.605
But this, again, is a really
rich and important area,

00:38:45.606 --> 00:38:48.100
and something where, again,
we have these large datasets.

00:38:48.101 --> 00:38:49.730
And sometimes they're
pretty well labeled,

00:38:49.731 --> 00:38:52.865
we know whether the services
are healthier or not.

00:38:52.866 --> 00:38:55.045
Coming back to that final point

00:38:55.046 --> 00:38:57.775
about Practitioner's Guide.

00:38:57.776 --> 00:38:59.875
I'm not a machine
learning person.

00:38:59.876 --> 00:39:03.080
I am a networking researcher
as well at heart,

00:39:03.081 --> 00:39:05.120
and the thing I've seen the most

00:39:05.121 --> 00:39:06.740
is what we call
the Knowledge Gap.

00:39:06.741 --> 00:39:08.460
There are some people who
know machine-learning,

00:39:08.461 --> 00:39:10.745
know the algorithms there
really, really well.

00:39:10.746 --> 00:39:12.930
I've got a bunch of
network scientists who

00:39:12.931 --> 00:39:15.405
know how A6 work and how A6 fail,

00:39:15.406 --> 00:39:17.265
and how to look at service data.

00:39:17.266 --> 00:39:19.780
The problem we have is when
people are more interested

00:39:19.781 --> 00:39:22.380
in their own field than they
are at solving the problem,

00:39:22.381 --> 00:39:24.165
these people can't
talk to each other,

00:39:24.166 --> 00:39:26.180
or they just fail to
talk to each other.

00:39:26.181 --> 00:39:28.600
So, for data science,

00:39:28.601 --> 00:39:30.250
the people who want to come
work with systems people,

00:39:30.251 --> 00:39:32.030
I think the key thing is having

00:39:32.031 --> 00:39:34.540
to become more interested in

00:39:34.541 --> 00:39:36.330
solving the problem and
learning more about

00:39:36.331 --> 00:39:39.215
the specific subject material,

00:39:39.216 --> 00:39:40.480
then in even knowing in

00:39:40.481 --> 00:39:42.400
the classification algorithms in

00:39:42.401 --> 00:39:43.720
order to make forward progress,

00:39:43.721 --> 00:39:46.310
to basically establish
what are the metrics,

00:39:46.311 --> 00:39:47.910
what are the salient dimensions,

00:39:47.911 --> 00:39:50.205
to be able to work through
the data and sort through it,

00:39:50.206 --> 00:39:52.605
to come up with better outcomes.

00:39:52.606 --> 00:39:55.020
I've experienced some
positive examples of this.

00:39:55.021 --> 00:39:56.640
I've experienced some negative
examples of this,

00:39:56.641 --> 00:39:57.750
and I'm glad to be part of

00:39:57.751 --> 00:40:01.080
the conversation here and
talk more. So, thank you.

00:40:06.680 --> 00:40:15.740
&gt;&gt; Okay, questions? Okay,
next up we have Keith.

00:40:15.741 --> 00:40:17.705
Keith is from Stanford,

00:40:17.706 --> 00:40:19.710
where he is an
Assistant Professor.

00:40:19.711 --> 00:40:24.980
His research is in
Network Systems and it mostly

00:40:24.981 --> 00:40:27.380
cuts across traditional
abstraction boundaries

00:40:27.381 --> 00:40:30.120
and uses statistical techniques.

00:40:30.121 --> 00:40:36.770
Keith has won the second
dissertation award.

00:40:37.830 --> 00:40:40.230
Prior to being a professor,

00:40:40.231 --> 00:40:44.245
he was a staff reporter at
the Wall Street Journal.

00:40:44.246 --> 00:40:46.650
He later worked at Ksplice,

00:40:46.651 --> 00:40:49.390
which was acquired by
Oracle where he was

00:40:49.391 --> 00:40:50.790
the Vice President of

00:40:50.791 --> 00:40:52.500
Product Management
and Business Development,

00:40:52.501 --> 00:40:53.830
and according to his webpage,

00:40:53.831 --> 00:40:56.775
he was also responsible
for cleaning the bathroom.

00:40:56.776 --> 00:40:59.535
Yes. Welcome, Keith.

00:40:59.536 --> 00:41:03.790
&gt;&gt; Thank you. I don't
know which input I'm

00:41:03.791 --> 00:41:09.170
on. I think he's working on it.

00:41:10.350 --> 00:41:17.335
Perfect. All right,
thank you very much.

00:41:17.336 --> 00:41:20.345
I'm not an expert in anything,

00:41:20.346 --> 00:41:21.830
but I was invited
to be on this panel

00:41:21.831 --> 00:41:23.625
so I'm going to do my best.

00:41:23.626 --> 00:41:27.590
I think I'm going
to do with biology,

00:41:27.591 --> 00:41:29.595
machine learning is good.

00:41:29.596 --> 00:41:31.440
One thing I want to highlight is

00:41:31.441 --> 00:41:32.770
that when people say
machine learning,

00:41:32.771 --> 00:41:34.270
they mean a bunch of
different things.

00:41:34.271 --> 00:41:35.120
I think there are maybe,

00:41:35.121 --> 00:41:37.090
at least three paradigms in
which we talk about using

00:41:37.091 --> 00:41:38.770
machine learning
in the context of

00:41:38.771 --> 00:41:40.770
deploying an operational system.

00:41:40.771 --> 00:41:44.795
So, Paradigm 1 is what I'll
call learn then deploy.

00:41:44.796 --> 00:41:45.950
So you learn something,

00:41:45.951 --> 00:41:47.415
machine-learning
produces an artifact,

00:41:47.416 --> 00:41:51.810
someone runs in a data center
on a million core GPU,

00:41:51.811 --> 00:41:54.380
all kinds of stuff, we
produce an awesome artifact,

00:41:54.381 --> 00:41:56.725
learned the network and then
we deploy that network.

00:41:56.726 --> 00:41:59.280
That's Paradigm 1. Paradigm 2,

00:41:59.281 --> 00:42:01.210
it's about learning in real life.

00:42:01.211 --> 00:42:03.835
We deploy something and
then we learn over time.

00:42:03.836 --> 00:42:05.520
Something's in
the field, we learn.

00:42:05.521 --> 00:42:07.980
Paradigm 3, I'm going to call,

00:42:07.981 --> 00:42:09.560
we learn from the machines.

00:42:09.561 --> 00:42:11.070
Not the computer learning,
but machine-learning

00:42:11.071 --> 00:42:12.820
teaches us about
our own thinking.

00:42:12.821 --> 00:42:14.300
Like these are
sort of three different ways

00:42:14.301 --> 00:42:14.800
we could talk about

00:42:14.801 --> 00:42:17.075
machine learning in the context
of network systems.

00:42:17.076 --> 00:42:19.125
I guess I have sort of
three different views.

00:42:19.126 --> 00:42:20.610
Paradigm 1, which I
think is currently

00:42:20.611 --> 00:42:23.155
the dominant one
people talk about.

00:42:23.156 --> 00:42:25.860
In my opinion, it's often
harder than we expect,

00:42:25.861 --> 00:42:28.390
that's not obvious but
it's often tricky.

00:42:28.391 --> 00:42:30.310
Paradigm 2, this
deploy and learn.

00:42:30.311 --> 00:42:31.820
They continuously
learning in the field.

00:42:31.821 --> 00:42:33.040
I think is quite valuable and I

00:42:33.041 --> 00:42:34.555
think we should research it,

00:42:34.556 --> 00:42:37.575
but it's hard because of
the nature of network systems.

00:42:37.576 --> 00:42:38.920
I think there's other domains

00:42:38.921 --> 00:42:39.770
where this is probably easier,

00:42:39.771 --> 00:42:41.200
but in networks is not so easy.

00:42:41.201 --> 00:42:43.455
I think we should be
working on those problems.

00:42:43.456 --> 00:42:45.240
Paradigm 3, this idea that we

00:42:45.241 --> 00:42:46.570
can learn from the experience

00:42:46.571 --> 00:42:49.200
of teaching a machine to
match us in some capability,

00:42:49.201 --> 00:42:50.450
I am not such old-fashioned,

00:42:50.451 --> 00:42:53.430
like 1960s view, but I think
it's still true today.

00:42:53.431 --> 00:42:54.920
I think we should talk
more in this term.

00:42:54.921 --> 00:42:56.700
So, I'm going try and talk
about it in that sense.

00:42:56.701 --> 00:42:58.510
So, Paradigm 1,
learn then deploy.

00:42:58.511 --> 00:43:00.420
Why do I say it's often
harder than we expect?

00:43:00.421 --> 00:43:02.650
Well, I have been bit by this.

00:43:02.651 --> 00:43:05.410
So, here's a paper
for me that was

00:43:05.411 --> 00:43:06.870
NSDI 2013 about

00:43:06.871 --> 00:43:09.130
a machine-learning
congestion control algorithm,

00:43:09.131 --> 00:43:10.660
uses Bayesian reasoning for

00:43:10.661 --> 00:43:12.835
congestion control,
the sprout algorithm.

00:43:12.836 --> 00:43:14.620
You can see on this graph,

00:43:14.621 --> 00:43:15.800
the best algorithms have

00:43:15.801 --> 00:43:18.200
the lowest delay and
the best throughput.

00:43:18.201 --> 00:43:20.550
So, the best algorithms
are all algorithm,

00:43:20.551 --> 00:43:22.865
sprout up into the right,
we're very proud of that.

00:43:22.866 --> 00:43:24.820
Now, what happens in real life?

00:43:24.821 --> 00:43:28.250
So we deploy this around
the world, and five years later,

00:43:28.251 --> 00:43:29.300
here's the result that was taken

00:43:29.301 --> 00:43:32.420
yesterday on the T-Mobile
network in California.

00:43:32.421 --> 00:43:34.140
Same kind of graph.
Here's sprout.

00:43:34.141 --> 00:43:35.700
So, still sort of
up into the right,

00:43:35.701 --> 00:43:37.110
but it's not really
as dominant as we

00:43:37.111 --> 00:43:39.080
showed in our paper
from five years ago.

00:43:39.081 --> 00:43:41.620
There's a whole bunch of
other algorithms that are on

00:43:41.621 --> 00:43:44.375
this Pareto frontier,
that's in America.

00:43:44.376 --> 00:43:46.715
This is the same place we
measured when we did our paper.

00:43:46.716 --> 00:43:48.020
Here's result that was literally

00:43:48.021 --> 00:43:49.210
this morning in New Delhi,

00:43:49.211 --> 00:43:50.880
India on the Airtel network.

00:43:50.881 --> 00:43:54.260
Here's my algorithm, it
is terrible, it sucks.

00:43:54.261 --> 00:43:55.890
Can I say that here?

00:43:55.891 --> 00:43:57.490
I don't know where,
okay, thank you.

00:43:57.491 --> 00:43:59.885
So, Yes this learn
then deploy I learned,

00:43:59.886 --> 00:44:01.250
I wrote a paper about it,

00:44:01.251 --> 00:44:02.505
the paper won an award,

00:44:02.506 --> 00:44:03.820
I got a professorship.

00:44:03.821 --> 00:44:05.950
Now, we learned in the field
that doesn't actually work,

00:44:05.951 --> 00:44:07.125
at least not outside America.

00:44:07.126 --> 00:44:09.225
This is not a good paradigm
for anyone other than me,

00:44:09.226 --> 00:44:11.310
I'm the beneficiary,
but not everyone else.

00:44:11.311 --> 00:44:13.115
So, that's my algorithm.

00:44:13.116 --> 00:44:14.560
Here's a different
algorithm Vivace,

00:44:14.561 --> 00:44:16.630
and this was published
in NSDI 2018.

00:44:16.631 --> 00:44:18.415
Here's the publication again.

00:44:18.416 --> 00:44:20.100
We see really good
performance here which

00:44:20.101 --> 00:44:22.285
shares the link is
flows enter and leave.

00:44:22.286 --> 00:44:24.360
This is in the paper.
Here's the result,

00:44:24.361 --> 00:44:26.210
again, this morning in Brazil.

00:44:26.211 --> 00:44:27.460
Same algorithm but again

00:44:27.461 --> 00:44:28.460
it's quite different
from the paper.

00:44:28.461 --> 00:44:30.280
We see that the first
flow dominates,

00:44:30.281 --> 00:44:32.800
the second flow much less,
the third flow much less.

00:44:32.801 --> 00:44:34.415
So, there's the difference
again between

00:44:34.416 --> 00:44:36.665
the learn and then the deploy.

00:44:36.666 --> 00:44:39.040
Here's a different paper,
this is the pensive paper

00:44:39.041 --> 00:44:41.630
that was at SIGCOMM, the
most recent SIGCOMM.

00:44:41.631 --> 00:44:44.690
This is a learned
scheme for deciding

00:44:44.691 --> 00:44:48.500
which video bit rate to
send a user over a network,

00:44:48.501 --> 00:44:50.250
where there's unpredictability
about the network,

00:44:50.251 --> 00:44:51.060
and whether they're
going to need to

00:44:51.061 --> 00:44:51.900
re-buffer and you want to

00:44:51.901 --> 00:44:53.270
have good quality video

00:44:53.271 --> 00:44:54.950
but you don't want to
run out of buffer,

00:44:54.951 --> 00:44:56.635
so this is in the paper,

00:44:56.636 --> 00:44:58.944
if you take the same
learned artifact,

00:44:58.945 --> 00:45:00.590
and you deploy it
over similar networks

00:45:00.591 --> 00:45:01.870
some students in my class tried

00:45:01.871 --> 00:45:03.670
and they got these results.

00:45:03.671 --> 00:45:06.580
So, again that learn it
works when you learn it,

00:45:06.581 --> 00:45:09.945
but later when you deploy
it, something's different.

00:45:09.946 --> 00:45:11.710
It doesn't work anymore.

00:45:11.711 --> 00:45:14.550
The only way to know
this is to teach

00:45:14.551 --> 00:45:16.400
a networking class where
you can compel students

00:45:16.401 --> 00:45:18.465
to reproduce papers, and
then they have to do it.

00:45:18.466 --> 00:45:20.540
It's not actually easy to
learn this information.

00:45:20.541 --> 00:45:22.470
We put a lot of
effort into deploying

00:45:22.471 --> 00:45:24.340
this global infrastructure
whose conclusion

00:45:24.341 --> 00:45:25.715
is that I was wrong,

00:45:25.716 --> 00:45:28.170
but if we hadn't done that we
would never know I would be

00:45:28.171 --> 00:45:30.995
going around blissfully
talking about how great I was.

00:45:30.996 --> 00:45:33.000
Here's another one, so BBR

00:45:33.001 --> 00:45:34.060
this is an algorithm from Google,

00:45:34.061 --> 00:45:35.760
this was published in ACM Queue,

00:45:35.761 --> 00:45:37.410
and they say BBR converges

00:45:37.411 --> 00:45:39.170
towards a fair share of
the bottleneck bandwidth,

00:45:39.171 --> 00:45:40.575
and worst-case,

00:45:40.576 --> 00:45:42.100
the oppositional algorithms might

00:45:42.101 --> 00:45:43.490
grab more than their fair share.

00:45:43.491 --> 00:45:45.340
BBR can have less
than its fair share.

00:45:45.341 --> 00:45:46.940
That's the claim in the paper,

00:45:46.941 --> 00:45:48.960
here is a slide
presentation from ripe

00:45:48.961 --> 00:45:50.700
76 just a few months ago,

00:45:50.701 --> 00:45:54.045
and the gentleman from APNIC
finds that's just not true.

00:45:54.046 --> 00:45:55.350
Here's a cubic flow and

00:45:55.351 --> 00:45:57.670
suddenly when
the BBR flow starts,

00:45:57.671 --> 00:45:59.615
it crushes the cubic flow.

00:45:59.616 --> 00:46:02.090
So, there's learn,
Google learned it,

00:46:02.091 --> 00:46:03.100
but then when you deploy it, you

00:46:03.101 --> 00:46:04.875
get something quite different.

00:46:04.876 --> 00:46:06.780
So, what they find is that BBR is

00:46:06.781 --> 00:46:08.100
not a scalable approach,

00:46:08.101 --> 00:46:09.480
it works great when

00:46:09.481 --> 00:46:11.570
just a few users use it
like one big company,

00:46:11.571 --> 00:46:13.300
but not when lots
of people use it,

00:46:13.301 --> 00:46:14.735
is BBR a failure,

00:46:14.736 --> 00:46:16.650
not necessarily because now
there's going to be a BBR

00:46:16.651 --> 00:46:19.390
2.0, we'll see what happens.

00:46:19.391 --> 00:46:21.720
Here's another example.
So, network system a

00:46:21.721 --> 00:46:23.885
broad field let's talk
what search engines.

00:46:23.886 --> 00:46:26.400
There's a lot of interest
in using the data

00:46:26.401 --> 00:46:29.055
from search engines to learn
things about the real world.

00:46:29.056 --> 00:46:33.100
In 2008, Google had a paper
about learning about the flu,

00:46:33.101 --> 00:46:35.165
from the search queries
that they got.

00:46:35.166 --> 00:46:36.550
The idea here is to learn,

00:46:36.551 --> 00:46:39.960
a model to predict
the real-world flu incidence by

00:46:39.961 --> 00:46:42.100
correlating search engine queries

00:46:42.101 --> 00:46:43.640
against government flu data.

00:46:43.641 --> 00:46:46.775
Which is released on
two to three week time lag.

00:46:46.776 --> 00:46:48.335
They learn the model,

00:46:48.336 --> 00:46:50.780
and then they deployed in
real-time to break flu in

00:46:50.781 --> 00:46:51.990
advance of the government to help

00:46:51.991 --> 00:46:53.725
direct vaccines more efficiently.

00:46:53.726 --> 00:46:55.010
So, this was on the front page of

00:46:55.011 --> 00:46:56.500
The New York Times,
aches, a sneeze,

00:46:56.501 --> 00:46:57.960
a Google search, it was a nature,

00:46:57.961 --> 00:46:59.335
that was on Charlie Rose,

00:46:59.336 --> 00:47:01.915
and Google put this plot
on their website,

00:47:01.916 --> 00:47:03.595
and you can see the predictor

00:47:03.596 --> 00:47:05.715
which is in blue
does really good,

00:47:05.716 --> 00:47:09.310
against the real life data
which is in yellow.

00:47:09.311 --> 00:47:12.695
This is what they put
on their website,

00:47:12.696 --> 00:47:15.250
they don't show you is this,

00:47:15.320 --> 00:47:17.390
it's actually the vast majority

00:47:17.391 --> 00:47:18.920
this plot is training data.

00:47:18.921 --> 00:47:21.030
So, this is what it
is but it's actually

00:47:21.031 --> 00:47:24.030
only a tiny bit of here
it was not trained on.

00:47:24.031 --> 00:47:25.550
So this is hard so
they learned it

00:47:25.551 --> 00:47:27.040
on this training data here,

00:47:27.041 --> 00:47:28.370
I annotated the figure,

00:47:28.371 --> 00:47:30.260
but then when they
actually deploy it,

00:47:30.261 --> 00:47:31.610
this is not deployment.

00:47:31.611 --> 00:47:33.910
It's they call it
historical estimates,

00:47:33.911 --> 00:47:35.160
but it wasn't deployed
historically,

00:47:35.161 --> 00:47:35.840
when they actually deploy,

00:47:35.841 --> 00:47:39.290
this is what happens.
It goes crazy.

00:47:39.291 --> 00:47:42.880
We don't know why, Google

00:47:42.881 --> 00:47:44.590
eventually they kept
trying to fix this and

00:47:44.591 --> 00:47:46.545
kept screwing up they
eventually gave up on it.

00:47:46.546 --> 00:47:48.140
We don't know why this happens,

00:47:48.141 --> 00:47:49.480
maybe someone in
the audience knows

00:47:49.481 --> 00:47:51.840
why, I don't know why.

00:47:52.010 --> 00:47:54.500
Here's another example,
Spam Filtering,

00:47:54.501 --> 00:47:55.690
and then another sort of broadly

00:47:55.691 --> 00:47:56.910
construed network
systems problems.

00:47:56.911 --> 00:47:58.880
SpamAssasins is a spam
filtering engine,

00:47:58.881 --> 00:48:00.630
has very clever
community approach,

00:48:00.631 --> 00:48:02.200
the idea is that
anyone can propose

00:48:02.201 --> 00:48:04.710
a spam filtering decision rule.

00:48:04.711 --> 00:48:07.660
It's okay because a central party

00:48:07.661 --> 00:48:10.230
learns the best weights
for each of these rules,

00:48:10.231 --> 00:48:11.500
a sort of a panel of experts.

00:48:11.501 --> 00:48:13.575
So, anyone can contribute
a putative expert.

00:48:13.576 --> 00:48:15.920
People learn, essentially
they learn the best rates.

00:48:15.921 --> 00:48:17.370
So, if you're expert is nonsense

00:48:17.371 --> 00:48:18.760
they'll just give it
a weight of zero.

00:48:18.761 --> 00:48:19.830
If it happens to be very

00:48:19.831 --> 00:48:21.370
predictive they'll give
it a strong weight.

00:48:21.371 --> 00:48:23.880
Those weights are then,
deployed in the field.

00:48:23.881 --> 00:48:26.480
So, in 2007 people
proposed various rules,

00:48:26.481 --> 00:48:28.150
some a rule, and the rule was,

00:48:28.151 --> 00:48:30.920
does the year matched to 200 and

00:48:30.921 --> 00:48:34.020
then any character, the date.

00:48:34.021 --> 00:48:35.730
It turned out that
caught a lot of spam,

00:48:35.731 --> 00:48:39.190
because a lot of spam is sent
for a date in the future.

00:48:39.191 --> 00:48:41.750
Like a date like 2012,

00:48:41.751 --> 00:48:42.790
which would happen
in the future so

00:48:42.791 --> 00:48:44.215
that would've been spam.

00:48:44.216 --> 00:48:45.840
So, this had an extremely low

00:48:45.841 --> 00:48:47.580
false positive rate
when they learned it.

00:48:47.581 --> 00:48:49.070
People can see why,
because it is in

00:48:49.071 --> 00:48:52.980
2007 that they're learning
it? So, what happened?

00:48:54.070 --> 00:48:57.280
January 1st, 2010 SpamAssassin

00:48:57.281 --> 00:49:00.010
started killing
every message spam.

00:49:00.011 --> 00:49:02.315
Physicists are very easy
to see case where did they

00:49:02.316 --> 00:49:04.500
learn then deployed it just
falls down in the real world.

00:49:04.501 --> 00:49:06.400
Because they had this idea

00:49:06.401 --> 00:49:07.800
that the algorithms
were black boxes,

00:49:07.801 --> 00:49:09.160
they didn't have to
know how they worked,

00:49:09.161 --> 00:49:11.140
they just looked at empirically
how well they worked,

00:49:11.141 --> 00:49:13.330
and they waited them according
to real performance,

00:49:13.331 --> 00:49:15.340
that did not work
when time evolved.

00:49:15.341 --> 00:49:17.300
Time is a very clear case here
if you depend on the date.

00:49:17.301 --> 00:49:19.420
As soon as you hit 2010
times is going to evolve.

00:49:19.421 --> 00:49:20.805
So, what was the fix for this?

00:49:20.806 --> 00:49:22.535
How do you fix this problem?

00:49:22.536 --> 00:49:23.715
&gt;&gt; You got change dates?

00:49:23.716 --> 00:49:24.645
&gt;&gt; What was that?

00:49:24.646 --> 00:49:25.295
&gt;&gt; You got change dates?

00:49:25.296 --> 00:49:27.070
&gt;&gt; Well, they just
changed it to 2020.

00:49:27.071 --> 00:49:28.440
That's their new decision rule.

00:49:28.441 --> 00:49:30.835
Yes. So, I think
the lesson here is

00:49:30.836 --> 00:49:33.310
that it is very easy
to fool yourself,

00:49:33.311 --> 00:49:34.480
and I think Feynman
says, "You are

00:49:34.481 --> 00:49:35.875
the easiest person to fool".

00:49:35.876 --> 00:49:37.250
This learn and deploy it is as

00:49:37.251 --> 00:49:38.630
a challenging parrot pattern,

00:49:38.631 --> 00:49:40.820
and empirically,
very very smart people,

00:49:40.821 --> 00:49:42.840
and very very smart
mega corporations

00:49:42.841 --> 00:49:45.260
embarrass themselves
going on television to,

00:49:45.261 --> 00:49:48.835
in a segue with premature
declarations to success.

00:49:48.836 --> 00:49:50.210
So, I think we have
to keep that in mind.

00:49:50.211 --> 00:49:51.840
So, paradigm two is okay let's

00:49:51.841 --> 00:49:53.760
deploy and learn over time.

00:49:53.761 --> 00:49:55.550
We'll build systems that
learn continuously,

00:49:55.551 --> 00:49:56.670
and we'll directly observe

00:49:56.671 --> 00:49:58.520
the operational figure
of merit over time.

00:49:58.521 --> 00:50:00.710
That way we can react quickly
to real-world changes.

00:50:00.711 --> 00:50:02.870
The real-world changes
just we just change.

00:50:02.871 --> 00:50:04.480
So, some people are
really good at this.

00:50:04.481 --> 00:50:05.600
So, here's a paper from Google,

00:50:05.601 --> 00:50:07.590
that quick paper at
SIGCOMM last year,

00:50:07.591 --> 00:50:09.140
we see Google directly

00:50:09.141 --> 00:50:10.800
observed everyday how
well quick is doing,

00:50:10.801 --> 00:50:12.905
and if there's a problem,
they can immediately fix it.

00:50:12.906 --> 00:50:14.610
Whether it's automatic,
or them they can fix it.

00:50:14.611 --> 00:50:16.930
This is really a world that
I would want to live in.

00:50:16.931 --> 00:50:18.620
It would be very comfortable
with deploying something

00:50:18.621 --> 00:50:19.700
crazy because you can see

00:50:19.701 --> 00:50:21.360
immediately how well it's doing.

00:50:21.361 --> 00:50:23.070
Not just congestion control but,

00:50:23.071 --> 00:50:25.330
in the real world we
do learn over time,

00:50:25.331 --> 00:50:26.615
if I'm driving a car,

00:50:26.616 --> 00:50:28.220
and something bad happens to me,

00:50:28.221 --> 00:50:29.355
I'm going to learn,
I'm going to say,

00:50:29.356 --> 00:50:30.620
"Wow, I'm definitely not going to

00:50:30.621 --> 00:50:32.610
get that close to
a Ferrari next time".

00:50:32.611 --> 00:50:34.165
You want to learn over time,

00:50:34.166 --> 00:50:36.390
you don't want to just
have a fixed mindset

00:50:36.391 --> 00:50:37.980
and do it continuously,

00:50:37.981 --> 00:50:40.020
if you build a robot
to change a diaper,

00:50:40.021 --> 00:50:41.905
and the baby wiggles out of it,

00:50:41.906 --> 00:50:43.270
you want to learn
from that experience,

00:50:43.271 --> 00:50:44.135
you don't want to be like,"Okay,

00:50:44.136 --> 00:50:45.410
I'm going to stay fixed".

00:50:45.411 --> 00:50:47.160
So, this continuous learning has

00:50:47.161 --> 00:50:49.670
some intuitive appeal,
but it's hard.

00:50:49.671 --> 00:50:51.375
It's hard on a network.

00:50:51.376 --> 00:50:53.470
There's some classic results
that make it hard in

00:50:53.471 --> 00:50:56.000
cases where information
is distributed.

00:50:56.001 --> 00:50:57.580
So, learning is it's
much easier when

00:50:57.581 --> 00:50:59.010
all the information
comes to one place,

00:50:59.011 --> 00:51:00.449
when information is distributed,

00:51:00.450 --> 00:51:02.310
it's really hard and
there's some old results,

00:51:02.311 --> 00:51:03.960
that are therefore profound that

00:51:03.961 --> 00:51:06.235
say that "In some cases
it's actually impossible".

00:51:06.236 --> 00:51:08.530
Impossibilities based group
decision-making with

00:51:08.531 --> 00:51:10.735
separate aggregation
of beliefs and values.

00:51:10.736 --> 00:51:12.830
So,if you keep
the information apart,

00:51:12.831 --> 00:51:14.715
It's really hard to learn
something consistent.

00:51:14.716 --> 00:51:16.540
If you bring all
the information in one place,

00:51:16.541 --> 00:51:17.170
you can do it.

00:51:17.171 --> 00:51:18.730
If you're keeping
information apart, which

00:51:18.731 --> 00:51:20.000
is what we'd like
to do on a network,

00:51:20.001 --> 00:51:23.065
we like to be decentralized,
It's hard to do.

00:51:23.066 --> 00:51:25.230
So, also have agents

00:51:25.231 --> 00:51:27.160
are adversarial it's very
hard to handle this,

00:51:27.161 --> 00:51:28.700
how do you handle
competing agents,

00:51:28.701 --> 00:51:29.300
one guy says, "Oh,

00:51:29.301 --> 00:51:30.810
I've learned this as best,
I learned this is best".

00:51:30.811 --> 00:51:32.870
These are hard problems. They
are hard network problems.

00:51:32.871 --> 00:51:34.000
They are problems
we should work on,

00:51:34.001 --> 00:51:35.495
but they're not easy.

00:51:35.496 --> 00:51:36.610
Michael Shapiro and I

00:51:36.611 --> 00:51:37.790
actually have
a conjecture that it's

00:51:37.791 --> 00:51:40.440
impossible for a decentralized
congestion control scheme

00:51:40.441 --> 00:51:43.160
that greedily optimizing
an objective function

00:51:43.161 --> 00:51:45.475
to be globally
asymptotically stable.

00:51:45.476 --> 00:51:47.180
That it's an unproved conjecture,

00:51:47.181 --> 00:51:49.545
but it may be hard in
these adversarial cases.

00:51:49.546 --> 00:51:51.405
How about in neural networks?

00:51:51.406 --> 00:51:52.910
Do we have to just
bring all the data to

00:51:52.911 --> 00:51:55.260
the Cloud and learn there,
or just learning the edge?

00:51:55.261 --> 00:51:56.330
It wouldn't it be nice my student

00:51:56.331 --> 00:51:57.935
John Evans is working
on this project,

00:51:57.936 --> 00:52:00.840
if the data in the computer
or in different places,

00:52:00.841 --> 00:52:02.680
to still be able to learn
without having to bring

00:52:02.681 --> 00:52:04.770
all the compute to the edge
or all the data in the Cloud.

00:52:04.771 --> 00:52:07.160
It'll be nice to now split
the problem somehow,

00:52:07.161 --> 00:52:09.250
and even learn continuously
between date and

00:52:09.251 --> 00:52:11.405
the edge and computing the Cloud
and learn between them.

00:52:11.406 --> 00:52:13.290
Unfortunately, so
far you can't do it.

00:52:13.291 --> 00:52:14.260
There's a green region we

00:52:14.261 --> 00:52:15.580
want to be in and
we're not in there.

00:52:15.581 --> 00:52:18.350
So, lesson two is
deploy-and-learn can be great,

00:52:18.351 --> 00:52:19.460
but it's hard on a network,

00:52:19.461 --> 00:52:20.500
when data's in different places,

00:52:20.501 --> 00:52:22.875
computes in different places,
and people are adversarial.

00:52:22.876 --> 00:52:25.800
So, I think to wrap up I think
I'm most excited about is

00:52:25.801 --> 00:52:26.940
this old fashioned view that

00:52:26.941 --> 00:52:29.405
the real benefit of teaching
something to a machine,

00:52:29.406 --> 00:52:31.650
is that you learn about
that thing yourself.

00:52:31.651 --> 00:52:33.740
We can tell the machine
design a system,

00:52:33.741 --> 00:52:34.920
and the machine comes, here

00:52:34.921 --> 00:52:36.060
my requirements, here my goals,

00:52:36.061 --> 00:52:38.275
machine comes back and
says, "How about this",

00:52:38.276 --> 00:52:40.310
it's better if it looks so weird,

00:52:40.311 --> 00:52:41.540
if we think that's crazy,

00:52:41.541 --> 00:52:43.820
but huh, it does meet
the requirements,

00:52:43.821 --> 00:52:45.605
and it does maximize
the objective,

00:52:45.606 --> 00:52:48.415
that's very interesting let
me clarify my thinking,

00:52:48.416 --> 00:52:51.105
that "Hmm" is like the most
exciting thing in science.

00:52:51.106 --> 00:52:53.795
So, teaching something is
the best way to learn anything,

00:52:53.796 --> 00:52:55.940
the dumber the student the
better the teacher will learn,

00:52:55.941 --> 00:52:57.830
because if you explained
yourself so clearly,

00:52:57.831 --> 00:52:59.355
machines are very dumb.

00:52:59.356 --> 00:53:00.700
So, teaching machines to

00:53:00.701 --> 00:53:02.090
learn to design systems
is the best way

00:53:02.091 --> 00:53:03.270
for us to learn about what it

00:53:03.271 --> 00:53:05.050
really means and what
we really care about.

00:53:05.051 --> 00:53:07.335
So, to wrap up here,
I think Paradigm one,

00:53:07.336 --> 00:53:09.420
it gets a lot of hype but it
is harder than we expect,

00:53:09.421 --> 00:53:11.075
and it is so easy
to fool yourself.

00:53:11.076 --> 00:53:13.420
Paradigm two, I think we
should be working on,

00:53:13.421 --> 00:53:14.450
I certainly I'm working on it,

00:53:14.451 --> 00:53:16.360
but the problems of
network systems,

00:53:16.361 --> 00:53:18.835
the decentralization of data
and compute makes it hard.

00:53:18.836 --> 00:53:20.960
And Paradigm three, I'm
quite sympathetic to,

00:53:20.961 --> 00:53:22.510
and I think even
if we never deploy

00:53:22.511 --> 00:53:24.060
anything that's the product
of Machine Learning,

00:53:24.061 --> 00:53:25.520
we should still do
Machine Learning

00:53:25.521 --> 00:53:27.990
because we learned so
much about the problem.

00:53:33.620 --> 00:53:36.195
&gt;&gt; So, we had a set of

00:53:36.196 --> 00:53:38.260
really interesting
perspectives on

00:53:38.261 --> 00:53:40.440
the topic of this panel.

