exactly how it works,

00:57:11.966 --> 00:57:14.330
how do I have confidence
that I could actually take

00:57:14.331 --> 00:57:15.900
action based on
its recommendations

00:57:15.901 --> 00:57:17.010
and not make things worse.

00:57:17.011 --> 00:57:18.290
And you'll all go
back to the adage.

00:57:18.291 --> 00:57:20.120
People have a tendency
to screw things up but

00:57:20.121 --> 00:57:22.600
to really make a big mess
it takes a computer.

00:57:22.601 --> 00:57:25.425
So, I am trying to hook up

00:57:25.426 --> 00:57:27.310
essentially machine
learning classification

00:57:27.311 --> 00:57:28.755
systems that are looking at data

00:57:28.756 --> 00:57:31.600
to automatic mitigation
mechanisms that will

00:57:31.601 --> 00:57:35.240
go in and change our network
to try and fix problems.

00:57:35.241 --> 00:57:37.630
That means is that if
that system runs a

00:57:37.631 --> 00:57:39.930
mock you could actually go
create a much bigger problem

00:57:39.931 --> 00:57:41.660
for our customers
than what actually

00:57:41.661 --> 00:57:44.425
existed there organically
in the network.

00:57:44.426 --> 00:57:48.864
So, for us it's been
this combination of guardrails.

00:57:48.865 --> 00:57:51.460
Around the behavior of this

00:57:51.461 --> 00:57:54.820
feedback loop essentially
so that no matter

00:57:54.821 --> 00:57:57.030
what the classifier says it

00:57:57.031 --> 00:57:58.270
thinks it should turn off

00:57:58.271 --> 00:57:59.345
in order to maybe get a problem.

00:57:59.346 --> 00:58:00.650
Remember that grid
failure example?

00:58:00.651 --> 00:58:02.140
If I can find the switch
I think is bad,

00:58:02.141 --> 00:58:04.030
I turn it off,
everything gets good.

00:58:04.031 --> 00:58:06.675
But if I turn off
all the switches, that's bad.

00:58:06.676 --> 00:58:09.520
So putting guardrails
around us we don't get

00:58:09.521 --> 00:58:12.090
kind of runaway behavior
and that if it looks

00:58:12.091 --> 00:58:15.360
like things are getting worse
and worse then we apply

00:58:15.361 --> 00:58:16.930
the brakes to the system
and actually start

00:58:16.931 --> 00:58:19.185
rolling back some of
the changes that we made.

00:58:19.186 --> 00:58:20.770
In my domain, at least,

00:58:20.771 --> 00:58:22.430
we have the ability
to sort of create

00:58:22.431 --> 00:58:23.480
a closed feedback loop and

00:58:23.481 --> 00:58:24.915
that's how we've approached it.

00:58:24.916 --> 00:58:26.520
Certainly our system
is recommended

00:58:26.521 --> 00:58:27.690
things to us that made

00:58:27.691 --> 00:58:29.300
no sense and sometimes

00:58:29.301 --> 00:58:31.315
there's right, sometimes
there's wrong.

00:58:31.316 --> 00:58:33.510
&gt;&gt; But one of the things I like

00:58:33.511 --> 00:58:36.730
about Dave's adage to error is

00:58:36.731 --> 00:58:38.950
human but to really foul
things up it requires

00:58:38.951 --> 00:58:42.220
a computer is that it
comes from the 1970's.

00:58:42.221 --> 00:58:44.260
They already knew back then.

00:58:44.261 --> 00:58:47.625
But when you talk about concerns

00:58:47.626 --> 00:58:49.330
about whether we have

00:58:49.331 --> 00:58:52.725
a system whose behavior we
don't really understand,

00:58:52.726 --> 00:58:55.280
I got to tell you
you're deluded if you

00:58:55.281 --> 00:58:56.970
think we understand
how the systems

00:58:56.971 --> 00:58:58.215
we've been building work.

00:58:58.216 --> 00:59:01.095
Okay. We already don't
understand them.

00:59:01.096 --> 00:59:02.730
It's just a question of degree.

00:59:02.731 --> 00:59:05.430
&gt;&gt; I think it's
a question of illusion.

00:59:05.431 --> 00:59:06.210
That is, we thought we're

00:59:06.211 --> 00:59:07.790
the students so that
we felt comfortable.

00:59:07.791 --> 00:59:09.110
&gt;&gt; But going back a little bit to

00:59:09.111 --> 00:59:10.675
the specifics of your question,

00:59:10.676 --> 00:59:11.950
there's this cultural question

00:59:11.951 --> 00:59:13.370
like "Do network
engineers want to

00:59:13.371 --> 00:59:14.700
give up control to something

00:59:14.701 --> 00:59:16.265
they don't understand
by a machine?"

00:59:16.266 --> 00:59:18.200
But on the other hand
if you actually

00:59:18.201 --> 00:59:20.795
look at RFCs that say
should and would,

00:59:20.796 --> 00:59:22.400
that kind of provides

00:59:22.401 --> 00:59:25.040
the framework and the
more of that that's in

00:59:25.041 --> 00:59:27.290
the RFC probably the better off

00:59:27.291 --> 00:59:29.680
you are because it says
your learning algorithm,

00:59:29.681 --> 00:59:31.070
it's only going to
have a few knobs

00:59:31.071 --> 00:59:32.630
that can change and you have

00:59:32.631 --> 00:59:36.510
some hope you're going
to understand it.

00:59:36.511 --> 00:59:40.080
So, that goes both ways.

00:59:40.340 --> 00:59:44.265
&gt;&gt; I think it's also
this composition.

00:59:44.266 --> 00:59:48.655
You can probably understand
something at a single level.

00:59:48.656 --> 00:59:51.600
It's like when you
have packets here and

00:59:51.601 --> 00:59:53.890
then there's protocols that

00:59:53.891 --> 00:59:55.920
are driving these packets
and then they rely on

00:59:55.921 --> 00:59:59.360
the arrival of acknowledgements
and that doesn't happen.

00:59:59.361 --> 01:00:01.080
Now, something at
this level triggers

01:00:01.081 --> 01:00:04.005
something at one level above
that trigger something.

01:00:04.006 --> 01:00:07.320
It's almost like a f of g of h

01:00:07.321 --> 01:00:09.975
of k sort of composing now.

01:00:09.976 --> 01:00:11.790
You only see the first thing and

01:00:11.791 --> 01:00:15.125
the last thing and you don't
know where it's breaking.

01:00:15.126 --> 01:00:19.430
That sort of stuff
is really hard.

01:00:19.431 --> 01:00:23.150
You can't isolate the fault very

01:00:23.151 --> 01:00:27.390
easily and that's what
frustrates us networking folks.

01:00:27.391 --> 01:00:30.890
We design with understanding
but unfortunately we

01:00:30.891 --> 01:00:35.360
compose things now and they
become big and complex.

01:00:36.100 --> 01:00:41.880
&gt;&gt; I think there's sort of

01:00:41.881 --> 01:00:44.130
a sense where if something has

01:00:44.131 --> 01:00:45.790
only a small number of parameters

01:00:45.791 --> 01:00:47.185
we thought that we understood it.

01:00:47.186 --> 01:00:49.170
Take the AI MD algorithm in TCP.

01:00:49.171 --> 01:00:52.295
You added an increase by
one, divide it by two.

01:00:52.296 --> 01:00:55.760
There's two parameters and
we wrote some nice proofs.

01:00:55.761 --> 01:00:58.950
And to then discover that
actually the flock behavior of

01:00:58.951 --> 01:01:02.210
a decentralized network
of computers running TCP,

01:01:02.211 --> 01:01:03.540
even with those
two parameters, is

01:01:03.541 --> 01:01:05.085
incredibly complicated.

01:01:05.086 --> 01:01:07.280
Has all kinds of fun
things that we discovered.

01:01:07.281 --> 01:01:09.730
It's actually
chaotic overdraft TLQ,

01:01:09.731 --> 01:01:11.285
and there's problems like

01:01:11.286 --> 01:01:12.700
incast but have to
do with the sort

01:01:12.701 --> 01:01:14.285
of packet by packet behavior

01:01:14.286 --> 01:01:16.330
that don't show up
in fluid models.

01:01:16.331 --> 01:01:19.140
So, even something with
just two parameters when run in

01:01:19.141 --> 01:01:21.100
a decentralized
networks situation

01:01:21.101 --> 01:01:23.575
is so fun but also so buggy.

01:01:23.576 --> 01:01:25.870
That's a sort of fun and
also humbling discovery.

01:01:25.871 --> 01:01:28.140
I think there's an appropriate
caution about deploying

01:01:28.141 --> 01:01:31.085
something with a 100 million
learn parameters.

01:01:31.086 --> 01:01:33.300
If we can't even get the AI and

01:01:33.301 --> 01:01:35.670
the MD understand those dynamics,

01:01:35.671 --> 01:01:37.475
I think we should
be appropriately

01:01:37.476 --> 01:01:38.780
humble about our ability

01:01:38.781 --> 01:01:42.070
to reason about a system
with a million parameters.

01:01:42.071 --> 01:01:46.150
Doing machine learning to
optimize tail behavior or

01:01:46.151 --> 01:01:48.740
worst-case behavior
especially in the presence

01:01:48.741 --> 01:01:52.205
of adversarial input is
an unsolved problem.

01:01:52.206 --> 01:01:54.120
It's one thing to optimize

01:01:54.121 --> 01:01:56.850
average-case behavior of an
image recognition program.

01:01:56.851 --> 01:01:58.940
And we know that
adversarial input

01:01:58.941 --> 01:02:00.210
to in image recognition

01:02:00.211 --> 01:02:03.300
is actually the adversary
has the better hand there.

01:02:03.301 --> 01:02:04.910
You can change
one pixel and suddenly

01:02:04.911 --> 01:02:07.920
a puppy turns into
the Sears Tower.

01:02:07.921 --> 01:02:12.000
So, I think there's

01:02:12.001 --> 01:02:15.549
an appropriate caution about

01:02:15.550 --> 01:02:17.885
increasing the number
of parameters.

01:02:17.886 --> 01:02:20.865
Defining a guardrail, which I
think is such a good point,

01:02:20.866 --> 01:02:22.530
I don't think we
know how to define

01:02:22.531 --> 01:02:25.040
the guardrails for
many decentralized scenarios.

01:02:25.041 --> 01:02:28.525
I mean, how do you
define stability?

01:02:28.526 --> 01:02:30.650
How do you define
a guardrail when you

01:02:30.651 --> 01:02:32.540
only observe part
of the situation?

01:02:32.541 --> 01:02:34.360
These are fun problems that we

01:02:34.361 --> 01:02:35.180
should be working on but I

01:02:35.181 --> 01:02:36.885
don't think we have the answers.

01:02:36.886 --> 01:02:38.760
&gt;&gt; Right. I think
an interesting question

01:02:38.761 --> 01:02:41.490
is whether adversarial attacks

01:02:41.491 --> 01:02:43.330
become more likely or less likely

01:02:43.331 --> 01:02:45.110
with machine learning system

01:02:45.111 --> 01:02:46.240
versus a hand crafted system.

01:02:46.241 --> 01:02:49.235
But I think let's hold
onto that question.

01:02:49.236 --> 01:02:50.400
I'd like to open

01:02:50.401 --> 01:02:51.720
the floor for questions
from the audience.

01:02:51.721 --> 01:02:54.390
Yes. Do we have
microphones? Okay.

01:02:56.780 --> 01:02:59.250
&gt;&gt; Thanks. Is this on?

01:02:59.251 --> 01:03:02.800
Okay. Tensor really has
interesting set of talks.

01:03:02.801 --> 01:03:05.750
My question is sort of more
broadly about the use of

01:03:05.751 --> 01:03:07.030
training datasets for

01:03:07.031 --> 01:03:09.305
machine learning
which is fundamental.

01:03:09.306 --> 01:03:11.880
Obvious problem with
such training datasets

01:03:11.881 --> 01:03:14.225
is that rare events are
by definition rare,

01:03:14.226 --> 01:03:16.040
and the kind of control systems

01:03:16.041 --> 01:03:17.780
that we need to put into
place for dealing with

01:03:17.781 --> 01:03:19.815
such events are not going to be

01:03:19.816 --> 01:03:22.770
perhaps even available
in the training dataset.

01:03:22.771 --> 01:03:24.550
So, there's
critical dependency on

01:03:24.551 --> 01:03:26.080
the training dataset and

01:03:26.081 --> 01:03:27.580
the fact that
these datasets may not

01:03:27.581 --> 01:03:29.730
include the cases that
you care about in

01:03:29.731 --> 01:03:33.180
practice makes reliance on

01:03:33.181 --> 01:03:35.650
machine learning only as

01:03:35.651 --> 01:03:39.990
your control system probably bad.

01:03:42.420 --> 01:03:44.660
&gt;&gt; I think that's a fair point.

01:03:44.661 --> 01:03:48.630
So, the kinds of
things that are in

01:03:48.631 --> 01:03:50.930
my examples that we're
trying to learn do

01:03:50.931 --> 01:03:53.990
actually happen a couple of
times a day across the fleet.

01:03:53.991 --> 01:03:55.420
So, they're rare but they

01:03:55.421 --> 01:03:57.010
happen often enough
that we actually have

01:03:57.011 --> 01:03:58.770
positive examples
and we can define

01:03:58.771 --> 01:04:00.640
a false-positive
false-negative rate on it.

01:04:00.641 --> 01:04:02.660
I still have to have
human engineers on call

01:04:02.661 --> 01:04:05.565
24/7 365 because
things will break

01:04:05.566 --> 01:04:08.790
that has never been
seen before and

01:04:08.791 --> 01:04:11.390
that any of the automatic
classification systems

01:04:11.391 --> 01:04:12.425
just don't know what to do with.

01:04:12.426 --> 01:04:14.915
Then we're back to
good old human troubleshooting.

01:04:14.916 --> 01:04:17.630
I mean, we talked with the ideas

01:04:17.631 --> 01:04:20.005
of trying to actually inject.

01:04:20.006 --> 01:04:22.080
When we have a partial
forward plant model

01:04:22.081 --> 01:04:23.440
and can predict if
this thing broke,

01:04:23.441 --> 01:04:24.480
what would it look like in

01:04:24.481 --> 01:04:26.010
terms of the symptoms we observe,

01:04:26.011 --> 01:04:28.970
trying to reject more essentially
synthetic failures into

01:04:28.971 --> 01:04:33.760
our training data, mixed success.

01:04:36.300 --> 01:04:38.840
&gt;&gt; Any other questions?

01:04:50.540 --> 01:04:54.530
&gt;&gt; I guess a question maybe
following up on Keath's talk.

01:04:54.531 --> 01:04:56.690
Do you think there's
a prospect for designing

01:04:56.691 --> 01:04:59.790
systems to be machine learnable?

01:05:01.040 --> 01:05:04.200
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:05:04.201 --> 01:05:05.100
&gt;&gt; And tell us how.

01:05:05.101 --> 01:05:10.860
&gt;&gt; Oh.

01:05:10.861 --> 01:05:11.220
&gt;&gt; I mean,

01:05:11.221 --> 01:05:19.010
&gt;&gt; Okay. Low systems
without a lot of

01:05:19.011 --> 01:05:21.500
high frequency response
are much easier to

01:05:21.501 --> 01:05:24.620
train a system for than
ones that are very twitchy.

01:05:24.621 --> 01:05:26.845
So, I think stability,

01:05:26.846 --> 01:05:29.775
slow evolution, and
behavior over time.

01:05:29.776 --> 01:05:33.205
Good network architecture,
good system architecture,

01:05:33.206 --> 01:05:35.030
whether you're trying to have
humans understand what's

01:05:35.031 --> 01:05:37.830
going on or an AI system
understand what's going on,

01:05:37.831 --> 01:05:40.245
they're all easier to control.

01:05:40.246 --> 01:05:42.170
&gt;&gt; We've understood
for a long time how to

01:05:42.171 --> 01:05:44.090
build congestion
control protocols

01:05:44.091 --> 01:05:47.635
that don't need machine
learning to operate well.

01:05:47.636 --> 01:05:49.710
We just don't build
those systems.

01:05:49.711 --> 01:05:52.260
For whatever reason, we

01:05:52.261 --> 01:05:54.690
use more complicated
mechanisms for

01:05:54.691 --> 01:05:57.570
coordination than we
really needed to just

01:05:57.571 --> 01:05:59.600
because man invented an algorithm

01:05:59.601 --> 01:06:01.530
that we all have
to live with now.

01:06:01.531 --> 01:06:04.220
So, there is
this question, I think,

01:06:04.221 --> 01:06:06.970
of if we have designed
for testability

01:06:06.971 --> 01:06:11.335
or modular design for
agility or whatever,

01:06:11.336 --> 01:06:14.790
do we need a set of
design principles

01:06:14.791 --> 01:06:17.900
for telling engineers how

01:06:17.901 --> 01:06:20.770
to build systems that can
actually be predictable?

01:06:20.771 --> 01:06:21.990
&gt;&gt; Sure. I think,

01:06:21.991 --> 01:06:23.060
they involve taking away the

01:06:23.061 --> 01:06:24.290
things that make
the problem hard.

01:06:24.291 --> 01:06:25.980
So, get rid of decentralization,

01:06:25.981 --> 01:06:27.640
get rid of adversaries,

01:06:27.641 --> 01:06:29.795
the problem becomes much easier.

01:06:29.796 --> 01:06:31.990
So, I can just control scheme

01:06:31.991 --> 01:06:34.320
where there is a central
dictator that governs

01:06:34.321 --> 01:06:36.430
the allocation of
everybody is much easier

01:06:36.431 --> 01:06:38.540
to reason about than
a Van Jacobson style scheme

01:06:38.541 --> 01:06:39.970
where there's a partially

01:06:39.971 --> 01:06:41.340
deliverable markup
decision process

01:06:41.341 --> 01:06:42.855
that's solved in
decentralize fashion.

01:06:42.856 --> 01:06:45.080
That is literally
an undecidable problem

01:06:45.081 --> 01:06:46.630
in general in computer science.

01:06:46.631 --> 01:06:49.925
So, that's why congestion control

01:06:49.926 --> 01:06:52.200
is so hard if you
do it decentralize.

01:06:52.201 --> 01:06:53.830
If you bring all
the information in

01:06:53.831 --> 01:06:55.880
one place and have a dictator
make the decision,

01:06:55.881 --> 01:06:57.465
it becomes much easier.

01:06:57.466 --> 01:06:59.100
&gt;&gt; But maybe observation is that

01:06:59.101 --> 01:07:00.580
there are many of
these kinds of tassels.

01:07:00.581 --> 01:07:02.000
For another one, it's
not centralization,

01:07:02.001 --> 01:07:04.170
decentralize but time to

01:07:04.171 --> 01:07:06.900
convergence or efficiency
and optimality.

01:07:06.901 --> 01:07:08.420
Sometimes giving up on some of

01:07:08.421 --> 01:07:10.110
those dimensions can actually

01:07:10.111 --> 01:07:11.440
get your system
that's going to be

01:07:11.441 --> 01:07:13.355
far easier to understand
or far simpler.

01:07:13.356 --> 01:07:15.020
But it is a trade-off.

01:07:15.021 --> 01:07:15.190
&gt;&gt; I agree.

01:07:15.191 --> 01:07:17.710
So, maybe there's
an intuitive feeling that the

01:07:17.711 --> 01:07:20.585
more you squeeze the
optimization level in one place,

01:07:20.586 --> 01:07:22.160
all the other things
are going to get worse.

01:07:22.161 --> 01:07:24.390
So, if you squeeze for
great average-case performance,

01:07:24.391 --> 01:07:26.090
probably your tails
and your liability

01:07:26.091 --> 01:07:28.010
and your adversary robustness,

01:07:28.011 --> 01:07:29.720
adversary in form of loosener
are going to get worse.

01:07:29.721 --> 01:07:32.880
So, maybe if we stop squeezing
for the thing that gets

01:07:32.881 --> 01:07:37.240
you promoted, then
we'll all be happier.

01:07:39.740 --> 01:07:42.700
I mean, being less glib.
Maybe if we somehow

01:07:42.701 --> 01:07:45.480
value these harder to define

01:07:45.481 --> 01:07:48.100
metrics instead of just your
average-case performance or

01:07:48.101 --> 01:07:49.300
95th percentile performance and

01:07:49.301 --> 01:07:50.955
stop squeezing so
hard on that one,

01:07:50.956 --> 01:07:53.230
maybe understandability
and stability,

01:07:53.231 --> 01:07:54.865
if we can quantify those,

01:07:54.866 --> 01:07:57.740
we might be able to
optimize for those instead.

01:07:58.280 --> 01:08:01.110
&gt;&gt; Also, the world of algorithms,

01:08:01.111 --> 01:08:03.135
there's better chance
to subsystems.

01:08:03.136 --> 01:08:06.250
There's more chance of this
being very helpful just

01:08:06.251 --> 01:08:07.750
because it's very clean and it

01:08:07.751 --> 01:08:09.725
got easier definition of what.

01:08:09.726 --> 01:08:11.065
If this input arrives,

01:08:11.066 --> 01:08:13.315
I want this desirable output.

01:08:13.316 --> 01:08:15.905
I think there you
can see success.

01:08:15.906 --> 01:08:18.900
&gt;&gt; It used to be one
of the requirements is

01:08:18.901 --> 01:08:20.940
good telemetry system
that actually gives you

01:08:20.941 --> 01:08:23.290
the data that you need
from actual deployment.

01:08:23.291 --> 01:08:26.190
I think search engines did
a great job because they

01:08:26.191 --> 01:08:27.620
had all the data and so we know

01:08:27.621 --> 01:08:29.365
exactly what it is that
we're clicking on and so on.

01:08:29.366 --> 01:08:30.780
Whereas, networking

01:08:30.781 --> 01:08:32.400
systems, I don't think
a lot of data comes

01:08:32.401 --> 01:08:34.190
back because it's all

01:08:34.191 --> 01:08:35.820
over the place and it's
expensive to bring it back.

01:08:35.821 --> 01:08:38.635
So, I think that's the challenge.

01:08:38.636 --> 01:08:40.600
I think we have
just a couple more minutes,

01:08:40.601 --> 01:08:44.560
so I want to ask
a final question.

01:08:47.870 --> 01:08:50.110
&gt;&gt; I think it's fair to say

01:08:50.111 --> 01:08:51.650
that overall the whole

01:08:51.651 --> 01:08:53.790
systems and networking
community has

01:08:53.791 --> 01:08:59.770
a cautionary approach to
infusing ML into the whole mix.

01:08:59.771 --> 01:09:01.650
It's fair. A lot of

01:09:01.651 --> 01:09:03.460
reasons were brought up
in all the presentations,

01:09:03.461 --> 01:09:04.930
we can outline that as well.

01:09:04.931 --> 01:09:07.395
My question to the panelists is,

01:09:07.396 --> 01:09:09.510
do you see any
sub-areas of systems

01:09:09.511 --> 01:09:11.440
and networking where we would

01:09:11.441 --> 01:09:16.260
actually be better off if
we lower the caution bar,

01:09:16.261 --> 01:09:18.470
and then the systems could

01:09:18.471 --> 01:09:21.260
evolve faster and for the better.

01:09:21.261 --> 01:09:23.370
I understand it's not
all the sub-areas but

01:09:23.371 --> 01:09:26.350
any specific sub-areas
you could think of?

01:09:26.480 --> 01:09:28.560
&gt;&gt; How about the scheduler or

01:09:28.561 --> 01:09:31.740
the branch predictor?
Those kinds of things.

01:09:32.180 --> 01:09:35.420
&gt;&gt; Why is it do you
think that lowering

01:09:35.421 --> 01:09:39.750
the caution bar in those is
better than say, condition?

01:09:39.800 --> 01:09:44.270
&gt;&gt; I think those are
examples perhaps where

01:09:44.271 --> 01:09:50.100
when there's a misprediction,

01:09:50.101 --> 01:09:52.060
the cost isn't so high.

01:09:52.061 --> 01:09:53.790
Now, David, he's worried

01:09:53.791 --> 01:09:55.970
about individual
packets being lost.

01:09:55.971 --> 01:09:57.830
That's not the right case

01:09:57.831 --> 01:09:58.900
then, you might
not want to do it.

01:09:58.901 --> 01:10:00.730
But if it's just a
question of, well,

01:10:00.731 --> 01:10:02.010
it's just going to
cost us a little more

01:10:02.011 --> 01:10:03.435
but we get the same performance

01:10:03.436 --> 01:10:06.950
or it's a microscopic change
in performance,

01:10:06.951 --> 01:10:09.205
you don't need to be as cautious.

01:10:09.206 --> 01:10:10.955
That's the low-hanging fruit.

01:10:10.956 --> 01:10:13.190
Especially, and you may
not need to know why

01:10:13.191 --> 01:10:15.410
it made the decision
because if it's wrong,

01:10:15.411 --> 01:10:17.250
it didn't cost you that much.

01:10:17.251 --> 01:10:19.440
&gt;&gt; There's totally
no way that I couldn't

01:10:19.441 --> 01:10:21.509
use quarks of our branch
prediction algorithm

01:10:21.510 --> 01:10:23.430
and speculative execution to

01:10:23.431 --> 01:10:25.755
steal information out of
a process or anything.

01:10:25.756 --> 01:10:28.290
That would totally never work.

01:10:28.291 --> 01:10:29.380
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:10:29.381 --> 01:10:30.260
I'm not sure it'd be worse if you

01:10:30.261 --> 01:10:31.150
used a neural network, though.

01:10:31.151 --> 01:10:33.500
I mean, how about
the query planner in a database?

01:10:33.501 --> 01:10:35.505
I think these kinds of things,

01:10:35.506 --> 01:10:38.170
we're already using sort
of folklore method,

01:10:38.171 --> 01:10:40.125
so why not use a neural network?

01:10:40.126 --> 01:10:41.880
&gt;&gt; Okay. I think
we need to close,

01:10:41.881 --> 01:10:44.355
but I just want you to
take 30 seconds each.

01:10:44.356 --> 01:10:46.725
Usually, we ask people
to predict the future,

01:10:46.726 --> 01:10:48.630
but I'm going to change
the question here.

01:10:48.631 --> 01:10:50.740
If you had that machine
learning technology and

01:10:50.741 --> 01:10:53.955
horsepower that we have
today in the '80s,

01:10:53.956 --> 01:10:56.070
when congestion control took

01:10:56.071 --> 01:10:58.440
shape or in the '90s
when BGP was designed

01:10:58.441 --> 01:11:01.155
or maybe the 2000s when

01:11:01.156 --> 01:11:03.160
firewalls and deep packet

01:11:03.161 --> 01:11:04.770
inspection and so on
came into the fold,

01:11:04.771 --> 01:11:06.910
how would things have been
done differently that

01:11:06.911 --> 01:11:08.780
would perhaps make
us better off today?

01:11:08.781 --> 01:11:12.580
If you had to
redesign things with

01:11:12.581 --> 01:11:15.045
machine learning technologies
available to you,

01:11:15.046 --> 01:11:17.200
what would have happened
differently? Just quick.

01:11:17.201 --> 01:11:19.270
&gt;&gt; Okay, I can go first. I'm just

01:11:19.271 --> 01:11:21.160
using the example as music thing.

01:11:21.161 --> 01:11:22.990
It learns the environment.

01:11:22.991 --> 01:11:24.900
So you come out on
off-the-shelf with

01:11:24.901 --> 01:11:26.790
an algorithm that works in

01:11:26.791 --> 01:11:28.630
many environments fairly well

01:11:28.631 --> 01:11:30.455
but not optimized for anything,

01:11:30.456 --> 01:11:32.920
can be, and then you
deploy in a particular,

01:11:32.921 --> 01:11:34.680
like in your indoor home area,

01:11:34.681 --> 01:11:36.310
you got some VR headset.

01:11:36.311 --> 01:11:38.410
That thing really
works differently in

01:11:38.411 --> 01:11:41.430
the basement room or
which one each work.

01:11:41.431 --> 01:11:43.710
I think that's an example
of something where

01:11:43.711 --> 01:11:46.760
it's adaptive to the environment.

01:11:46.761 --> 01:11:55.120
&gt;&gt; I think that if we'd had
this horsepower in the '80s,

01:11:55.121 --> 01:11:56.710
I would've been able to say,

01:11:56.711 --> 01:11:58.990
"Okay, Google, why is
the network so slow?"

01:11:58.991 --> 01:12:02.620
It would say, "I'm sorry. I
can't help you with that."

01:12:04.290 --> 01:12:06.655
That's the kind of
thing it's good at.

01:12:06.656 --> 01:12:08.200
It's recognizing patterns.

01:12:08.201 --> 01:12:11.630
It can understand my question,

01:12:11.631 --> 01:12:14.170
but it doesn't know the answer.

01:12:14.270 --> 01:12:19.575
&gt;&gt; Anyone else?
Congestion control.

01:12:19.576 --> 01:12:20.990
&gt;&gt; Okay, fine.
Congestion control.

01:12:20.991 --> 01:12:22.200
The truth is that people

01:12:22.201 --> 01:12:23.360
have been talking about
congestion control

01:12:23.361 --> 01:12:25.790
in the context of machine
learning since it started.

01:12:25.791 --> 01:12:28.400
I mean, Kashev's paper on
packet pair it talks about

01:12:28.401 --> 01:12:30.150
the then prevailing paradigm
of machine learning,

01:12:30.151 --> 01:12:32.610
which was fuzzy logic.
That's right, right?

01:12:32.611 --> 01:12:34.460
Yes, I mean, it's all about
fuzzy logic which was

01:12:34.461 --> 01:12:36.595
the upper hype thing
in the early '90s.

01:12:36.596 --> 01:12:38.850
I bet if deep neural networks
have been around then,

01:12:38.851 --> 01:12:40.120
you would have read
the exact same paper,

01:12:40.121 --> 01:12:42.000
but you'd have subbed
out fuzzy logic for

01:12:42.001 --> 01:12:43.270
neural networks but nothing would

01:12:43.271 --> 01:12:44.450
change about the algorithm.

01:12:44.451 --> 01:12:46.495
So, I don't know.

01:12:46.496 --> 01:12:50.040
&gt;&gt; Okay. The last one.

01:12:50.041 --> 01:12:52.105
&gt;&gt; Yes. I'm not really
coming up with one either.

01:12:52.106 --> 01:12:53.260
The one thing I do
know is whenever

01:12:53.261 --> 01:12:54.690
Skynet tries to take
over the world,

01:12:54.691 --> 01:12:57.970
it will probably crash because
of a networking problem.

01:12:57.971 --> 01:13:01.630
&gt;&gt; Okay. On that note, let's
thank our panel again.

